docs/architecture/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-architecture.md:206:Phase 7 verifier jobs use the shared workflow database and write `verification_jobs` rows, but verifier outcomes still flow through the proposal runtime rather than direct memory mutation. `mark_verified`, `mark_stale`, and `mark_invalidated` remain proposal kinds so replay, review, and event emission keep one mutation contract instead of giving verification a parallel write path.
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T49.log:1679:**Spec anchor:** [§Verification Engine](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#8-verification-engine), [§Phase 7: Verification And Freshness](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-7-verification-and-freshness)
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T49.log:1684:This task implements the second verification check from spec §Verification Engine: when a memory's evidence captured an exact text span (a byte range or `(start_line, end_line)`) at the time of creation, verification must confirm the current file content at that span still matches what was captured. If it does not, the verifier emits a `MarkStale` proposal (the assertion may still be true but the citation is stale) or `MarkInvalidated` (the evidence is gone). This is the difference between "the file still exists" (T48) and "what the memory said about the file is still backed by the same bytes." It is the load-bearing mechanism for the spec's §Stale Memory Leakage risk control.
