els/`; vector store backed by USearch at `.lattice/vector_index/`.\n42\t- **Intelligence:** workflow tools `prepare_change`, `plan_edit`, `get_context_capsule`, `expand_context`, `get_working_set_context`, `trace_scenario`, `summarize_subsystem`, `impact_from_diff`, `diagnose_failure`, `record_workflow_outcome`.\n43\t- **Structured memory:** verification status (`unverified` → `in_review` → `verified` / `stale` / `contradicted` / `superseded`), freshness policy (`session_scoped` | `branch_scoped` | `repo_scoped`), assertion types, provenance/evidence JSON blobs — documented in `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-structured-memory.md`.\n44\t- **Stable handles:** `SymbolId { file, name, byte_offset }` already used by `expand_context` — documented in `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-stable-follow-up-handles.md`.\n45\t\n46\t### MCP / RPC\n47\t\n48\t- JSON-RPC 2.0 over stdio (`crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/server.rs`).\n49\t- 43 registered tools dispatched through `McpHandler::handle_tools_call()` (`rpc/mcp.rs`).\n50\t- Context-handle cache (`rpc/context_cache.rs`) and per-session telemetry (`rpc/session_metrics.rs`).\n51\t\n52\t### Extension (TypeScript)\n53\t\n54\t- `extension/src/`: `extension.ts` (activation + 14 commands), `daemon.ts` (RPC client), `sidebar.ts` (webview with delivery-mix + handle-reuse meters), `docsWorkbench.ts` (docs graph), `codelens.ts`, `hover.ts`, `statusbar.ts`.\n55\t- Compile via `cd extension && npm run compile`; lint via `npm run lint`; tests via `npm test` (`node ./out/test/runTest.js`).\n56\t- Activity bar contribution (`viewsContainers.activitybar.lattice`) is the slot the new review UI will extend.\n57\t\n58\t### Tests\n59\t\n60\t- `cd daemon && cargo test --workspace` runs all crates.\n61\t- In-tree `tests.rs` next to each module is the convention (e.g. `storage/tests.rs`, `memory/tests.rs`, `intelligence/agent_tests.rs`).\n62\t- Benchmark fixtures live under `crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/benchmark_tests.rs` and `crates/lattice-core/src/query/benchmark_tests.rs`.\n63\t\n64\t### Architecture docs already present\n65\t\n66\t- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-structured-memory.md`\n67\t- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-stable-follow-up-handles.md`\n68\t- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-patch-oriented-planning.md`\n69\t- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-scenario-tracing.md`\n70\t- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-storage-and-search-backends.md`\n71\t\n72\tReference these from new design notes; do not duplicate their content.\n73\t\n74\t---\n75\t\n76\t## 3. Gap inventory (what the spec needs that does not yet exist)\n77\t\n78\t| Spec section | Status in current codebase | Build action |\n79\t|---|---|---|\n80\t| **Phase 0** — fork-or-extend decision + baseline benchmarks | Not done | Decision doc, baseline benchmark capture |\n81\t| **Phase 1** — unified identity for files, docs, sections, events, memories, handles | Partial: only `SymbolId` is stable today | Generalize identity, add resolver, ambiguity diagnostics |\n82\t| **Phase 2** — append-only event log + 20 event kinds + compaction | Absent | New `events` + `event_payloads` tables, writer/reader API, MCP capture, compaction snapshots |\n83\t| **Phase 3** — memory graph with first-class links, evidence, accesses, scores; `CounterMemory` class | Memory is single-table today | New `memory_links`, `memory_evidence`, `memory_accesses`, `memory_scores`; class taxonomy expansion; migration from existing rows |\n84\t| **Phase 4** — Retrieval V1 with intent classifier, anchor resolver, hybrid candidates, explainable scoring | Heuristic intent + keyword/semantic in `query/engine.rs`; no diagnostic reasons | New retrieval pipeline returning inclusion reasons + expansion handles |\n85\t| **Phase 5** — explicit working memory + checkpoints | Implicit prompt assembly today | New `working_memory_checkpoints` table + operations + MCP surface |\n86\t| **Phase 6** — consolidation engine (deterministic + LLM-driven proposals, manual queue, replay-safe) | Absent | New `consolidation_jobs` table, proposal-only writes, LLM provenance |\n87\t| **Phase 7** — verification engine (existence, span, scope, expiry, incremental) | `is_stale` flag exists; no incremental verifier | New verifier + `verification_jobs`; scope-leak prevention; stale-label discipline |\n88\t| **Phase 8** — Workflow Engine V2 + 10 new memory tools (audited for surface size) | Old tools exist; new memory tools absent | Redesign existing tools + add audited memory toolset |\n89\t| **Phase 9** — metrics + golden benchmark suite | Session metrics exist; no golden tasks | New benchmark fixture repo + CLI report |\n90\t| **Phase 10** — Human Review UI (memory inbox, queues, event/retrieval views) | Absent (sidebar shows stats only) | New webviews + JSON-RPC plumbing |\n91\t| **Phase 11** — hardening + full documentation suite | Partial | Large-repo perf, concurrency, recovery, corruption, migration tests; complete doc suite |\n92\t\n93\t---\n94\t\n95\t## 4. Fork-or-extend decision (spec §Phase 0 gate)\n96\t\n97\tThe spec's gate conditions for forking are: breaking `memories` table changes incompatible with migration, identity primary-key changes invalidating indexes, event log requiring incompatible SQLite/WAL changes, or two-or-more of the above.\n98\t\n99\tCurrent evidence suggests **extend on a long-lived branch** is feasible:\n100\t\n101\t- The existing `memories` schema already accommodates most of the new fields; new tables (`memory_links`, `memory_evidence`, `memory_accesses`, `memory_scores`) can be added alongside.\n102\t- `SymbolId` already gives stable identity; generalizing to other node families is additive.\n103\t- The event log can be a new SQLite file (`.lattice/events.db`) without disturbing `graph.db` or `memories.db`.\n104\t\n105\t**The fork-or-extend decision is itself the first taspace-fork-build/tasks.json","offset":998,"limit":285},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":450,"cache_read_input_tokens":47238,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":450,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":47,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012n7S8LWZzY1cvitTHPzudV","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"6e1e0b3d-59f0-45b5-814f-93ff7d5e5a5b","request_id":"req_011Cb7Mkg7mEy6vQ2d3xbFwx","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 8+9+Gate task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T37.log:2038:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2691:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:547:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WhKM9reGdKdCKZb74cYSmh","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Build Context — Cognitive Workspace Fork\n2\t\n3\t**Spec:** [`../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md`](../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md)\n4\t**Build directory:** `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/`\n5\t**Date assembled:** 2026-05-16\n6\t\n7\tThis file briefs the build harness on what the existing Lattice platform already provides, what the spec demands that it does not yet provide, and the engineering invariants every task must honor.\n8\t\n9\t---\n10\t\n11\t## 1. What the spec actually demands\n12\t\n13\tThe spec describes a **workspace cognition substrate** with three first-class sources of truth — **workspace graph**, **event log**, **memory graph** — composed under one daemon with explicit identity, retrieval, working memory, consolidation, verification, MCP surface, and human-review layers.\n14\t\n15\tThe implementation plan in the spec is structured as **eleven sequential phases** (Phase 0 → Phase 11). The implementation slice ordering (event log → memory graph → retrieval → consolidation → verification) is binding: identity must precede the event log, the event log must precede consolidation, and verification must precede retrieval taking memory as trusted. The build harness honors that sequence through `depends_on` edges in `tasks.json`.\n16\t\n17\t**Non-negotiables called out by the spec (Section \"Non-Negotiable Product Properties\"):**\n18\t\n19\t- Local-first by default; no silent broad workspace reads bypassing ignore rules.\n20\t- Deterministic identities for every file, symbol, doc section, event, and memory.\n21\t- Every durable memory has evidence; every retrieved memory has an inclusion reason.\n22\t- Every stale or contradicted memory is surfaced — never hidden behind recency.\n23\t- Every workflow bundle is compact by default; expansion is deliberate via stable handles.\n24\t- Every consolidation pass is recoverable, replayable, observable, and reversible.\n25\t- No unbounded payload growth, graph traversal, or event-log scan on hot paths.\n26\t- Hot-path budgets: identity resolution ≤2ms P99; event write ≤5ms P99 on `prepare_change` / `get_context_capsule` (spec §Phase 1, §Phase 2 DoD).\n27\t- LLM-driven consolidation jobs must produce proposals (never silent writes), record model + prompt hash + response hash, run only in background or manual-review modes, and operate under bounded queue depth with documented cost budgets (spec §Phase 6, \"LLM-driven consolidation\").\n28\t- Event log compaction snapshots are daemon-managed background operations, not manual; snapshot format is versioned and independently readable (spec §Storage Design).\n29\t\n30\t---\n31\t\n32\t## 2. Carry-forward inventory (what Lattice already gives us)\n33\t\n34\t### Daemon (Rust)\n35\t\n36\t- **Crates:** `daemon/crates/lattice-core/`, `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/`.\n37\t- **Graph:** `petgraph::DiGraph<GraphNode, EdgeKind>` in `crates/lattice-core/src/graph/`; nine edge kinds today (`Calls`, `Imports`, `Implements`, `Extends`, `TypeRef`, `Contains`, `LinksTo`, `Mentions`, `CoChanges`). Will be extended to the families listed in spec §Workspace Graph.\n38\t- **Parsers:** tree-sitter for TypeScript, Python, Rust, Go, Java, Markdown under `crates/lattice-core/src/parser/`.\n39\t- **Indexer:** incremental file → graph indexer with cached `ParsedFile` map and one-shot graph rebuild.\n40\t- **Storage:** SQLite under `.lattice/` with `graph.db` (nodes, edges, file_index, parsed_files) and `memories.db` (rich 80+ column `memories` table with `memories_fts`).\n41\t- **Embeddings:** ONNX runtime, 384-dim L2-normalized vectors loaded from `.lattice/models/`; vector store backed by USearch at `.lattice/vector_index/`.\n42\t- **Intelligence:** workflow tools `prepare_change`, `plan_edit`, `get_context_capsule`, `expand_context`, `get_working_set_context`, `trace_scenario`, `summarize_subsystem`, `impact_from_diff`, `diagnose_failure`, `record_workflow_outcome`.\n43\t- **Structured memory:** verification status (`unverified` → `in_review` → `verified` / `stale` / `contradicted` / `superseded`), freshness policy (`session_scoped` | `branch_scoped` | `repo_scoped`), assertion types, provenance/evidence JSON blobs — documented in `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-structured-memory.md`.\n44\t- **Stable handles:** `SymbolId { file, name, byte_offset }` already used by `expand_context` — documented in `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-stable-follow-up-handles.md`.\n45\t\n46\t### MCP / RPC\n47\t\n48\t- JSON-RPC 2.0 over stdio (`crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/server.rs`).\n49\t- 43 registered tools dispatched through `McpHandler::handle_tools_call()` (`rpc/mcp.rs`).\n50\t- Context-handle cache (`rpc/context_cache.rs`) and per-session telemetry (`rpc/session_metrics.rs`).\n51\t\n52\t### Extension (TypeScript)\n53\t\n54\t- `extension/src/`: `extension.ts` (activation + 14 commands), `daemon.ts` (RPC client), `sidebar.ts` (webview with delivery-mix + handle-reuse meters), `docsWorkbench.ts` (docs graph), `codelens.ts`, `hover.ts`, `statusbar.ts`.\n55\t- Compile via `cd extension && npm run compile`; lint via `npm run lint`; tests via `npm test` (`node ./out/test/runTest.js`).\n56\t- Activity bar contribution (`viewsContainers.activitybar.lattice`) is the slot the new review UI will extend.\n57\t\n58\t### Tests\n59\t\n60\t- `cd daemon && cargo test --workspace` runs all crates.\n61\t- In-tree `tests.rs` next to each module is the convention (e.g. `storage/tests.rs`, `memory/tests.rs`, `intelligence/agent_tests.rs`).\n62\t- Benchmark fixtures live under `crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/benchmark_tests.rs` and `crates/lattice-core/src/query/benchmark_tests.rs`.\n63\t\n64\t### Architecture docs already present\n65\t\n66\t- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-structured-memory.md`\n67\t- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-stable-follow-up-handles.md`\n68\t- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-patch-oriented-planning.md`\n69\t- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-scenario-tracing.md`\n70\t- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-storage-and-search-backends.md`\n71\t\n72\tReference these from new design notes; do not duplicate their content.\n73\t\n74\t---\n75\t\n76\t## 3. Gap inventory (what the spec needs that does not yet exist)\n77\t\n78\t| Spec section | Status in current codebase | Build action |\n79\t|---|---|---|\n80\t| **Phase 0** — fork-or-extend decision + baseline benchmarks | Not done | Decision doc, baseline benchmark capture |\n81\t| **Phase 1** — unified identity for files, docs, sections, events, memories, handles | Partial: only `SymbolId` is stable today | Generalize identity, add resolver, ambiguity diagnostics |\n82\t| **Phase 2** — append-only event log + 20 event kinds + compaction | Absent | New `events` + `event_payloads` tables, writer/reader API, MCP capture, compaction snapshots |\n83\t| **Phase 3** — memory graph with first-class links, evidence, accesses, scores; `CounterMemory` class | Memory is single-table today | New `memory_links`, `memory_evidence`, `memory_accesses`, `memory_scores`; class taxonomy expansion; migration from existing rows |\n84\t| **Phase 4** — Retrieval V1 with intent classifier, anchor resolver, hybrid candidates, explainable scoring | Heuristic intent + keyword/semantic in `query/engine.rs`; no diagnostic reasons | New retrieval pipeline returning inclusion reasons + expansion handles |\n85\t| **Phase 5** — explicit working memory + checkpoints | Implicit prompt assembly today | New `working_memory_checkpoints` table + operations + MCP surface |\n86\t| **Phase 6** — consolidation engine (deterministic + LLM-driven proposals, manual queue, replay-safe) | Absent | New `consolidation_jobs` table, proposal-only writes, LLM provenance |\n87\t| **Phase 7** — verification engine (existence, span, scope, expiry, incremental) | `is_stale` flag exists; no incremental verifier | New verifier + `verification_jobs`; scope-leak prevention; stale-label discipline |\n88\t| **Phase 8** — Workflow Engine V2 + 10 new memory tools (audited for surface size) | Old tools exist; new memory tools absent | Redesign existing tools + add audited memory toolset |\n89\t| **Phase 9** — metrics + golden benchmark suite | Session metrics exist; no golden tasks | New benchmark fixture repo + CLI report |\n90\t| **Phase 10** — Human Review UI (memory inbox, queues, event/retrieval views) | Absent (sidebar shows stats only) | New webviews + JSON-RPC plumbing |\n91\t| **Phase 11** — hardening + full documentation suite | Partial | Large-repo perf, concurrency, recovery, corruption, migration tests; complete doc suite |\n92\t\n93\t---\n94\t\n95\t## 4. Fork-or-extend decision (spec §Phase 0 gate)\n96\t\n97\tThe spec's gate conditions for forking are: breaking `memories` table changes incompatible with migration, identity primary-key changes invalidating indexes, event log requiring incompatible SQLite/WAL changes, or two-or-more of the above.\n98\t\n99\tCurrent evidence suggests **extend on a long-lived branch** is feasible:\n100\t\n101\t- The existing `memories` schema already accommodates most of the new fields; new tables (`memory_links`, `memory_evidence`, `memory_accesses`, `memory_scores`) can be added alongside.\n102\t- `SymbolId` already gives stable identity; generalizing to other node families is additive.\n103\t- The event log can be a new SQLite file (`.lattice/events.db`) without disturbing `graph.db` or `memories.db`.\n104\t\n105\t**The fork-or-extend decision is itself the first task (T01).** It is binding for every subsequent task: `depends_on` chains assume the decision documents which repo layout, crate boundaries, and migration policy all subsequent work targets. The harness does not assume the answer.\n106\t\n107\t---\n108\t\n109\t## 5. Shared standards every task must satisfy\n110\t\n111\t| Standard | Location | Where it applies |\n112\t|---|---|---|\n113\t| Cadres coding standard | `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md` | All code tasks: hard limits (file ≤800 lines, function ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10), single source of truth, error handling, naming, no broken windows, schema parity, tests as documentation, observability. |\n114\t| UI specification | `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/ui-specification.md` | All frontend tasks (Phase 10 review UI): every interactive surface follows the spec; no `window.confirm/prompt/alert`, modals via `<dialog>`, i18n via `i18n.t(...)`. |\n115\t| Definition of done | `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/definition-of-done-checklist.md` | The final readiness review task asserts every applicable checklist item is satisfied or explicitly waived. |\n116\t| Lattice project rules | `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/AGENTS.md` | Build/deploy procedure (`pkill -f lattice && cp daemon/target/release/lattice extension/bin/ && cp daemon/target/release/lattice ~/.vscode/extensions/lattice.lattice-0.1.0/bin/`), Markdown heading references for doc-anchored behavior, backward-compatibility discipline. |\n117\t| Execution philosophy | `/home/pete/.claude/CLAUDE.md` \"Execution Philosophy\" | No deferrals, no workarounds, no half-finished work, enterprise-grade on day one. The fork is implemented end-to-end across all eleven phases. |\n118\t\n119\t---\n120\t\n121\t## 6. Verification discipline\n122\t\n123\tEvery task in `tasks.json` carries `verification_commands` that are real shell commands run from the repo root. Conventions:\n124\t\n125\t- **Doc tasks** verify file existence and presence of required headings via `test -f` + `grep -q \"## Heading\"` chains.\n126\t- **Backend tasks** verify with `cd daemon && cargo build --release` and `cd daemon && cargo test --workspace -- <scope>`. New crate or module tests are pinned to the precise test path (`cargo test -p lattice-core --lib events::tests`) so failures map to a single task.\n127\t- **Schema/migration tasks** include a `cargo test` invocation that exercises the migration on a temp DB, plus a `sqlite3` schema-dump diff against a checked-in golden schema where the spec demands it.\n128\t- **Frontend tasks** verify with `cd extension && npm run compile` and `npm run lint`. Smoke tasks add `npm test`.\n129\t- **Cross-layer contract gates** include MCP schema tests (`cargo test --workspace -- mcp_schema`) and extension/daemon round-trip tests that exercise the new tool surface end-to-end.\n130\t- **Benchmark tasks** include `cargo bench` or a CLI invocation that emits a metrics report file the verifier can `test -f`.\n131\t- **Review tasks** verify by file existence of a structured findings report under `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/RNN.md` with the required sections.\n132\t\n133\tIf a task's verification cannot be expressed as a real command, the task is split or rewritten until it can.\n134\t\n135\t---\n136\t\n137\t## 7. Sequencing rationale\n138\t\n139\tThe eleven spec phases collapse into the following review groupings:\n140\t\n141\t1. **Foundation review** after Phase 0 — fork/extend decision, baseline benchmarks, crate boundary plan, architecture overview.\n142\t2. **Backend reviews** after Phases 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8 — one per substrate layer.\n143\t3. **Contract gates** at three boundaries: (a) Identity + Event Log + Memory cross-layer, (b) Memory + Verification + Retrieval cross-layer, (c) MCP tool surface schema regression, (d) Extension ↔ Daemon review-UI plumbing.\n144\t4. **Metrics review** after Phase 9.\n145\t5. **Frontend review** after Phase 10.\n146\t6. **Tests + Docs review** after Phase 11.\n147\t7. **Definition-of-Done gate** asserting every shared standard is satisfied.\n148\t8. **Final readiness review** asserting the system is production-grade for repeated agent use.\n149\t\n150\tNo phase is allowed to defer scope from a later phase. No phase ships a workaround when the real implementation is reachable in the same build.\n151\t\n152\t---\n153\t\n154\t## 8. Out of scope (explicitly named so it is not silently dropped)\n155\t\n156\tThe spec lists no items as out of scope. Therefore every section under spec §Implementation Plan, §Storage Design, §MCP Tool Contract Principles, §Documentation Requirements, §Testing Requirements, §Measurable Success Criteria, and §Risks is in scope for this build. The first-implementation-slice ordering is a *priority hint*, not a scope reduction.\n157\t\n158\tThe plan honors that: phases 0 through 11 are each materialized as tasks. The \"First Implementation Slice\" steps map to the earliest tasks in their respective phases — they are sequenced first inside their phase, but every phase ships in full before the build completes.\n159\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XjrTa6YAS1dmMW989ZPauZ","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"2286115f-f5be-472c-a8a6-d87768c1eefa","timestamp":"2026-05-17T02:33:24.491Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 10+11+Final task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T37.log:2070:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2726:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:711:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_016MBNff4Vmm8f1myrXiWNYC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WwRjPQsgfzTC4J4X1QNmPX","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T59.md","content":"# T59 — Phase 8 — New memory tools: verify_memory + explain_memory (unified) + list_memory_conflicts\n\n**Phase:** 8\n**Type:** backend\n**Model class:** balanced\n**Depends on:** R55\n**Spec anchor:** [§MCP Surface](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#9-mcp-surface)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard; Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task introduces the unified `verify_explain_memory` MCP tool (collapsing the spec's `verify_memory` + `explain_memory` per §MCP Surface \"Tool surface discipline\") and the new `list_memory_conflicts` tool. The unified tool runs verification checks against a memory and returns both the resulting status (`verified` | `unverified` | `in_review` | `stale` | `contradicted` | `superseded` | `expired` | `invalidated`) and a structured explanation of why each linked file/symbol/doc/test contributed to that status. `list_memory_conflicts` surfaces all contradiction and supersession edges anchored on a target memory or graph node so an operator can review them. Both tools are required by spec §MCP Surface and are the trust surface for the verification engine — without them, the Phase 7 verifier produces statuses that no assistant or operator can interrogate.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"New or redesigned memory tools: …`verify_memory`, `explain_memory`, `list_memory_conflicts`…\" (spec §MCP Surface)\n\n> \"Tool surface discipline: …audit whether `verify_memory` + `explain_memory` can be unified. The goal is the smallest surface that covers all assistant workflows. Consolidate before stabilizing the MCP contract.\" (spec §MCP Surface)\n\n> \"Verification outputs: `verified`, `unverified`, `in_review`, `stale`, `contradicted`, `superseded`, `expired`, `invalidated`.\" (spec §Verification Engine)\n\n> \"Every memory-bearing response should identify: why the memory was included; evidence strength; verification status; freshness status; contradiction/supersession state; scope; expansion target.\" (spec §MCP Tool Contract Principles)\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs: R55 (Phase 7 verification engine review) — `verify_explain_memory` is the MCP surface for the Phase 7 verifier; its outputs must match the Phase 7 status taxonomy exactly.\n- Existing repo files this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/verification/` — Phase 7 verifier (existence, span, scope, expiry checks; incremental verification jobs).\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory/` — Phase 3 memory store + links + evidence + accesses + scores.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/` — Phase 2 event writer (emits `MemoryRetrieved`, `MemoryInvalidated`).\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs` — MCP tool registration.\n  - Phase 1 identity types.\n  - T58 shared `MemoryRecord` type.\n- Standards: Cadres coding standard §Hard limits, §Single source of truth, §No broken windows, §Schema parity, §Observability; Lattice rules on Markdown heading references; `AGENTS.md` backward-compatibility discipline.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read spec §MCP Surface and §Verification Engine in full. Quote the \"Tool surface discipline\" paragraph verbatim into a doc comment on `memory_v2/verify_explain_memory.rs` so the rationale for unifying `verify_memory` + `explain_memory` is preserved at the call site.\n2. Implement `verify_explain_memory` in `memory_v2/verify_explain_memory.rs` as the unified tool. Accept `memory_id: MemoryId` plus optional `mode: \"verify\" | \"explain\" | \"verify_and_explain\"` (default `verify_and_explain`). Internally run the Phase 7 verifier on the memory, then construct an `ExplainReport` that itemizes each verification check (linked file exists, linked symbol exists, cited doc exists, linked test exists, evidence span still matches, contradiction state coherent, scope leak absent, time-bound expiry honored) and the outcome per check. Return a `VerifyExplainResponse` with `status: VerificationStatus`, `checks: Vec<CheckResult>` (each carrying `kind`, `target`, `outcome`, `evidence_ref`), `confidence_delta`, and an `expansion_handle` so the caller can pull richer context.\n3. Mode semantics: `verify` returns only the status + a minimal summary; `explain` skips re-running checks and returns the last persisted `ExplainReport`; `verify_and_explain` runs verification and returns the freshly-derived `ExplainReport`. Default is `verify_and_explain` because spec §Verification Engine and §MCP Tool Contract Principles together require fresh, explainable status on every memory surface.\n4. Implement `list_memory_conflicts` in `memory_v2/list_memory_conflicts.rs`. Accept `anchor: MemoryId | FileId | SymbolId | DocSectionId`, traverse `contradicts` and `supersedes` link types from Phase 3 memory_links, and return a paginated list of `ConflictRecord`s each carrying `source`, `target`, `link_type` (`contradicts` | `supersedes`), `link_strength`, `created_by`, `created_at`, `link_verification_status`, and `reason`. Honor scope filters per spec §Risks \"Scope Leakage.\"\n5. Both tools must emit appropriate Phase 2 events: `verify_explain_memory` emits `MemoryRetrieved` plus `MemoryInvalidated` when status transitions away from `verified`; `list_memory_conflicts` emits `MemoryRetrieved` for each surfaced conflict.\n6. Both tools must support compact + full + diagnostic render modes per spec §MCP Tool Contract Principles. Compact mode for `verify_explain_memory` returns status + one-line per failed check; diagnostic mode returns the full check trace.\n7. Register the two tools in `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs`. Per the T02 compatibility policy, the spec's nominal `verify_memory` and `explain_memory` names continue to respond as deprecation shims that forward to `verify_explain_memory` with the appropriate `mode`. The shims emit a deprecation warning in the response.\n8. In `memory_v2/verify_explain_tests.rs`, write tests that: (a) round-trip each tool's request/response through serde; (b) assert `verify_explain_memory` returns the exact Phase 7 status taxonomy and never invents a new status string; (c) assert every check result carries an evidence reference; (d) assert a memory whose linked symbol was deleted transitions to `stale` with a `linked_symbol_missing` check failure; (e) assert `list_memory_conflicts` returns both `contradicts` and `supersedes` edges with correct directionality; (f) assert scope-leak negative tests; (g) assert the `verify_memory` and `explain_memory` deprecation shims continue to respond and forward correctly.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/memory_v2/verify_explain_memory.rs` — unified verify+explain tool with `mode` parameter, full check trace, and expansion handle, per spec §MCP Surface tool-surface discipline.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/memory_v2/list_memory_conflicts.rs` — `list_memory_conflicts` traversing contradiction + supersession links with scope-aware pagination.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/memory_v2/verify_explain_tests.rs` — unit + scope-leak negative tests covering schema, status taxonomy parity with Phase 7, evidence-reference completeness, conflict directionality, and shim forwarding.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/memory_v2/verify_explain_memory.rs` — confirms the unified verify+explain file exists at the harness path.\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/memory_v2/list_memory_conflicts.rs` — confirms the `list_memory_conflicts` file exists at the harness path.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-daemon --lib rpc::memory_v2::verify_explain_tests` — every verify/explain/conflicts test passes (schema, status parity, evidence completeness, conflict directionality, scope-leak negatives, shim forwarding).\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file.\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10.\n- Schema parity: `VerifyExplainResponse`, `CheckResult`, `ConflictRecord` derive `Serialize`, `Deserialize`, `Debug`, `Clone`; every public field has a doc comment; the `mode` enum is serde-tagged with snake_case wire names; the `VerificationStatus` enum reuses the Phase 7 type rather than declaring a parallel one.\n- No silent error handling — every verifier failure returns a typed error; scope-leak rejections are explicit errors, not empty results.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code; no unjustified `#[allow(...)]`.\n- Public API names follow intent-over-mechanism naming (`CheckResult::evidence_ref`, not `CheckResult::ref_str`).\n- For SQLite: all queries go through Phase 3 store and Phase 7 verifier APIs; no raw SQL in the RPC layer.\n- Observability: each tool emits `tracing::info!` spans with `tool`, `memory_id`, `scope`, `status`, `check_count` fields.\n- Doc citation: cite spec headings `## MCP Surface`, `## Verification Engine`, and `## MCP Tool Contract Principles` in module doc comments.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: the unified `verify_explain_memory` ships in this task; the spec's two-tool API is collapsed before MCP contract stabilization per §MCP Surface.\n- [ ] If a backward-incompatible change was needed, the AGENTS.md \"Backward-compatibility and contract discipline\" section was followed; the `verify_memory` + `explain_memory` deprecation shims continue to respond per the T02 compatibility policy.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4764,"cache_read_input_tokens":68448,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4764,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":59,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":{"cache_miss_reason":{"type":"unavailable"}},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012n7S8LWZzY1cvitTHPzudV","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"6925f9b8-658b-4628-9f96-5512a5babaa4","request_id":"req_011Cb7MzXVz1xvApfw62RZzy","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 8+9+Gate task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T37.log:2085:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2741:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:806:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_015fKcvZfgcEs97BQgrrZ5xL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Y7QFPHstAz9FnuCrEEM8Ld","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T65.md","content":"# T65 — Phase 9 — Metric collection module (all required signals from spec)\n\n**Phase:** 9\n**Type:** backend\n**Model class:** balanced\n**Depends on:** R64\n**Spec anchor:** [§Phase 9: Metrics And Evaluation](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-9-metrics-and-evaluation)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard; Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task creates the canonical metrics module that collects every assistant-usefulness signal required by spec §Phase 9. Without a single module owning these signals, downstream tasks (T66 benchmark fixtures, T67 MCP surface, T68 CLI report, T69 regression tests) would each invent their own definitions and diverge. The module turns the event log + memory access history + workflow outcomes into the nine required signals named in spec §Phase 9: tool calls per successful task, irrelevant files opened per task, relevant anchor recall, memory inclusion precision, memory later-used rate, stale memory surfaced rate, contradiction missed rate, tests recommended versus tests needed, and workflow success after first plan.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Required metrics: tool calls per successful task; irrelevant files opened per task; relevant anchor recall; memory inclusion precision; memory later-used rate; stale memory surfaced rate; contradiction missed rate; tests recommended versus tests needed; workflow success after first plan.\" (spec §Phase 9)\n\n> \"Phase 9 Deliverables: retrieval relevance metrics; memory usefulness metrics; context token efficiency metrics; stale/contradicted memory surfacing metrics; test-pass-after-plan metrics; repeated-failure reduction metrics; benchmark tasks with golden expected anchors; regression dashboard or CLI report.\"\n\n> \"Definition of done: changes to retrieval, memory, and workflow behavior can be evaluated without relying on anecdote.\" (spec §Phase 9)\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs: R64 (MCP contract gate) — metric collection depends on a stable tool surface so signal definitions reference canonical tool names from T61.\n- Existing repo files this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/` — Phase 2 event reader; all metrics derive from event aggregation.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory/` — Phase 3 memory accesses, scores, evidence; used for memory-related signals.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/verification/` — Phase 7 verification statuses; used for stale-surfaced and contradiction-missed signals.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/session_metrics.rs` — current per-session telemetry; T65 supersedes its partial coverage with a complete spec-aligned set.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/record_workflow_outcome.rs` and the T62 outcome recorder — source for workflow success signals.\n  - Phase 1 identity types (every signal references stable IDs, never names).\n- Standards: Cadres coding standard `## Hard limits`, `## Single source of truth`, `## No broken windows`, `## Schema / model parity`, `## Observability`, `## Tests as documentation`.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read spec §Phase 9 in full. Quote the \"Required metrics\" paragraph verbatim into a doc comment on `metrics/signals.rs` so the canonical list is preserved at the call site.\n2. Create `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/metrics/mod.rs` exporting `signals`, the `MetricSignal` enum, the `MetricValue` struct, and the `MetricsCollector` aggregator. Doc-comment the module with a reference to spec §Phase 9.\n3. In `metrics/signals.rs`, define `pub enum MetricSignal` with one variant per spec-required signal: `ToolCallsPerSuccessfulTask`, `IrrelevantFilesOpenedPerTask`, `RelevantAnchorRecall`, `MemoryInclusionPrecision`, `MemoryLaterUsedRate`, `StaleMemorySurfacedRate`, `ContradictionMissedRate`, `TestsRecommendedVsNeeded`, `WorkflowSuccessAfterFirstPlan`. Derive `Serialize`, `Deserialize`, `Clone`, `Copy`, `Debug`, `PartialEq`, `Eq`, `Hash`. Implement `MetricSignal::as_str()` returning the canonical snake_case wire name used by the T67 MCP surface and the T68 CLI report.\n4. Define `pub struct MetricValue { signal: MetricSignal, value: Option<f64>, denominator: Option<u64>, sample_count: u64, source: MetricSource, computed_at: DateTime<Utc>, reason_if_null: Option<String> }`. The `Option<f64>` value supports honest reporting when no data is available — never fabricate zeros. The `source` field discriminates `EventLog`, `MemoryStore`, `WorkflowOutcome`, `Verifier`, `SessionMetrics`.\n5. In `metrics/signals.rs`, implement a deterministic collector function per signal that aggregates from the appropriate source(s):\n   - `ToolCallsPerSuccessfulTask` — count `ToolCalled` events per `task_id` whose terminal event is `WorkflowSucceeded`; mean across tasks in scope.\n   - `IrrelevantFilesOpenedPerTask` — count `FileRead` events per task where the file was not part of the eventual `PatchApplied` or `WorkflowSucceeded` artifact set.\n   - `RelevantAnchorRecall` — fraction of golden anchors (from T66 fixtures) that appear in the workflow's `ContextBundleReturned` anchor list.\n   - `MemoryInclusionPrecision` — fraction of `MemoryRetrieved` entries that were subsequently referenced (`MemoryExpanded`, cited by `PatchApplied`, or referenced by a later `WorkflowSucceeded`).\n   - `MemoryLaterUsedRate` — fraction of retrieved memories that the consumer used downstream within the same session or a subsequent reuse window.\n   - `StaleMemorySurfacedRate` — fraction of `MemoryRetrieved` entries whose verification status was `stale` and that lacked a stale label in the response payload.\n   - `ContradictionMissedRate` — fraction of memory inclusions where a contradiction link existed but was not surfaced.\n   - `TestsRecommendedVsNeeded` — comparison between `find_relevant_tests` output and the test set actually required to detect the patch (golden answer from T66).\n   - `WorkflowSuccessAfterFirstPlan` — fraction of tasks where the first `PlanCreated` event led to `WorkflowSucceeded` without a `WorkflowFailed` in between.\n6. Implement `pub struct MetricsCollector { … }` exposing `collect(scope: MetricScope, signals: &[MetricSignal]) -> Vec<MetricValue>`. `MetricScope` mirrors the §Memory Graph scope taxonomy (`session | branch | repo | user | organization`) plus an optional time range.\n7. Honor the spec §Non-Negotiable Product Properties bounds: no unbounded event-log scan on hot paths. Every collector applies a scope filter and a configurable maximum sample size, with explicit `incomplete: true` on the `MetricValue` when truncated.\n8. In `metrics/signals_tests.rs`, write tests that: (a) every `MetricSignal` variant has a deterministic collector; (b) collectors return `None` with a reason when no data is available rather than fabricating zero; (c) each collector returns the spec-defined value on a hand-crafted event fixture; (d) scope filters prevent cross-scope leakage; (e) sample-size bounds are honored.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/metrics/mod.rs` — module root exporting `signals`, `MetricSignal`, `MetricValue`, `MetricSource`, `MetricsCollector`, `MetricScope`; doc-comment cites spec §Phase 9.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/metrics/signals.rs` — `MetricSignal` enum with all nine spec-required variants, `MetricValue` struct, deterministic collector implementations, scope and sample-size bounds.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/metrics/signals_tests.rs` — unit tests covering deterministic collectors, honest null reporting, fixture-derived correct values, scope-filter enforcement, sample-size bounds.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/metrics/mod.rs` — confirms the module root file exists at the harness path.\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/metrics/signals.rs` — confirms the signals file exists at the harness path.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-core --lib metrics::signals_tests` — every metric-signal test passes (deterministic collectors, honest null, fixture correctness, scope filters, sample-size bounds).\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file (split `signals.rs` per signal family if it grows beyond, e.g. `signals/tool_usage.rs`, `signals/memory.rs`).\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10.\n- Schema parity: `MetricSignal`, `MetricValue`, `MetricSource`, `MetricScope` derive `Serialize`, `Deserialize`, `Debug`, `Clone`; every public field has a doc comment; wire names match the strings used by T67/T68.\n- No silent error handling — missing data produces `None` with a `reason_if_null` string, never a fabricated zero.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code; no unjustified `#[allow(...)]`.\n- Public API names follow intent-over-mechanism naming (`MetricSignal::RelevantAnchorRecall`, not `MetricSignal::AnchorRec`).\n- For SQLite: queries go through Phase 2/3/7 APIs; no raw SQL in the metrics module.\n- Observability: collectors emit `tracing::debug!` spans with `signal`, `scope`, `sample_count`, `incomplete` fields.\n- Doc citation: cite spec headings `## Phase 9: Metrics And Evaluation`, `## Event Log`, `## Memory Graph`, `## Verification Engine` in module doc comments.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: every one of the nine spec §Phase 9 \"Required metrics\" signals is collected by this task — no signal is punted to T67 or T68.\n- [ ] If a backward-incompatible change was needed, the AGENTS.md \"Backward-compatibility and contract discipline\" section was followed; the existing `session_metrics` module retains its current API as a deprecation shim until T67 wires the canonical surface through MCP.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3516,"cache_read_input_tokens":92137,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3516,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":51,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012n7S8LWZzY1cvitTHPzudV","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"3cf115cb-be58-4e98-bfce-c64ff2fe93eb","request_id":"req_011Cb7NM9YiaUaTnV73t6JP7","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 8+9+Gate task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T37.log:2095:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2752:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:893:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015qAoQeXM7HFtFDzBbPkUNq","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All 17 task brief files written successfully.\n\n**Files written: 17**\n\nFirst 3 paths confirmed:\n- `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T39.md`\n- `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T40.md`\n- `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T41.md`\n\nAll 17 files exist (T39, T40, T41, T42, T43, T44, T45, T46, R47, T48, T49, T50, T51, T52, T53, R54, R55) under `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/`.\n\nEach brief follows the prior-agent template (Goal, Spec excerpts that drive this task, Inputs, Steps with 5-10 concrete steps, Files expected, Verification with exact commands from tasks.json, Coding-standard checks citing the ≤800-line/≤50-line/≤3-nesting/≤10-cyclomatic limits and no-broken-windows rules, Definition of done checklist).\n\nKey task-specific points encoded as required:\n- **T39**: proposal-only model, `consolidation_jobs` + `consolidation_proposals` tables, bounded queue drops with warn log.\n- **T40**: synchronous deterministic episode template only, ≤5ms hot-path budget, LLM-forbid guard.\n- **T41**: deterministic scanners (duplicates, supersession, demotion, stale-marker, refresh) all routing through T39 proposals; `stale_marker.rs` + `refresh.rs` ship in this task per spec bullets.\n- **T42**: four LLM jobs with mode guard forbidding synchronous mode; malformed responses leave memory unchanged and emit `ConsolidationFailed` event.\n- **T43**: SHA-256 prompt/response hashes, per-kind queue bounds, budget catalog with fallback strategies, and the architecture doc at `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-consolidation-llm-budgets.md`.\n- **T44**: scope-gated manual review queue for repo + organization.\n- **T45**: replay-safe execution, reversal, cached-provenance replay (live LLM never called).\n- **T46**: integration tests covering proposals, apply/reject, replay, queue bounds, failure events.\n- **R47**: review with required headings (`## Spec alignment`, `## Coding-standard alignment`, `## Proposal discipline`, `## LLM budget evidence`, `## Findings`, `## Verdict`).\n- **T48**: eight-state output model verbatim, file/symbol/doc/test existence, `verification_jobs` table.\n- **T49**: exact-span content hashing, encoding-flip distinguished from content change, no whole-file reads.\n- **T50**: `ScopeFilter` enforced at store boundary, negative tests for all four scopes, `MemoryScopeFiltered` event.\n- **T51**: time-bound expiry, incremental verification narrowed to changed files/symbols, bounded work budget, snapshot-id advance on success.\n- **T52**: `SurfacingPipeline::partition` enforcing trusted vs advisory split, exhaustive `classify` match, ranker hard precondition excluding non-`Verified`.\n- **T53**: integration tests proving stale never trusted + scope leakage blocked across all eight states and all four scopes.\n- **R54**: review with required headings (`## Spec alignment`, `## Coding-standard alignment`, `## Stale-label discipline`, `## Scope-leak evidence`, `## Findings`, `## Verdict`).\n- **R55**: cross-layer contract gate with required headings; ships new test file `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/contract_tests/memory_verification_retrieval.rs` per tasks.json `files_expected`.\n\nNo code was implemented; tasks.json, context.md, and plan.md were not modified."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a163eb6f432e922f5 (use SendMessage with to: 'a163eb6f432e922f5' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 121791\ntool_uses: 37\nduration_ms: 848530</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"569d5d02-ce86-46eb-9622-7ea8cc55e105","timestamp":"2026-05-17T02:46:35.675Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are writing markdown task brief files for a build harness. Do NOT implement the feature itself — just write the brief markdown files.\n\nRepo root: /home/pete/cadres/lattice\nSpec: /home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md  (READ Phases 6 and 7 sections + §Consolidation Engine + §Verification Engine especially \"LLM-driven consolidation\" subsection)\nContext: /home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/context.md\nTasks JSON: /home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks.json\nShared standards: /home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md\n\nWrite these 16 files at `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/<TASK_ID>.md`:\n\nT39, T40, T41, T42, T43, T44, T45, T46, R47, T48, T49, T50, T51, T52, T53, R54, R55\n\nThat's 17 files — write all of them.\n\n**Use the same template structure as prior agents** (Goal, Spec excerpts, Inputs, Steps, Files expected, Verification, Coding-standard checks, Definition of done).\n\n**Task-specific guidance:**\n\n- T39 — consolidation job runtime: proposal model means NO silent writes; every LLM-driven job produces a proposal record requiring explicit apply or reject. Tables: `consolidation_jobs`. Queue is bounded (drops new jobs when full per spec, with log warning).\n- T40 — synchronous session consolidation: episode summaries on small task traces only. NOT on hot path (per spec §Phase 2 DoD ≤5ms budget).\n- T41 — deterministic jobs: duplicate detection, supersession candidates, demote unused/low-value memories, mark stale memories after graph changes, refresh memories whose evidence still matches.\n- T42 — LLM-driven jobs: episode summary, procedure extraction from repeated successful workflows, contradiction detection, failure-pattern extraction. MUST run only in background or manual-review modes, never on synchronous post-task hot path.\n- T43 — LLM provenance: each proposal records model, prompt hash, response hash. Queue depth bounded. Cost and latency budgets documented per job type in `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-consolidation-llm-budgets.md`. Failed/malformed responses leave prior memory unchanged and emit consolidation failure event.\n- T44 — manual review queue for high-scope (repo + organization) memory changes.\n- T45 — replay-safe execution: jobs can be replayed; provenance + prior state preserved.\n- T46 — integration tests: proposals, apply/reject, replay, queue bounds, failure events.\n- R47 — Phase 6 review. Required headings: `## Spec alignment`, `## Coding-standard alignment`, `## Proposal discipline`, `## LLM budget evidence`, `## Findings`, `## Verdict`.\n- T48 — verification engine core: file/symbol/doc/test existence checks. Outputs from spec §Verification Engine: verified/unverified/in_review/stale/contradicted/superseded/expired/invalidated.\n- T49 — exact-span evidence validation: when evidence captured an exact span, validate the span text still matches.\n- T50 — branch/workspace scope enforcement (deny leakage). Negative tests required.\n- T51 — time-bound expiry + incremental verification triggered by graph changes (large repos cannot tolerate full rescans).\n- T52 — stale surfacing in workflow bundles + label discipline: stale or contradicted memory cannot appear as trusted guidance. Non-negotiable from spec §Non-Negotiable Product Properties.\n- T53 — integration tests: stale never displayed as trusted; scope leakage blocked.\n- R54 — Phase 7 review. Required headings: `## Spec alignment`, `## Coding-standard alignment`, `## Stale-label discipline`, `## Scope-leak evidence`, `## Findings`, `## Verdict`.\n- R55 — contract gate Memory ↔ Verification ↔ Retrieval. Required headings: `## Contract surfaces`, `## Round-trip evidence`, `## Stale-label round-trip`, `## Scope-filter round-trip`, `## Findings`, `## Verdict`. Adds new test file `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/contract_tests/memory_verification_retrieval.rs` per tasks.json `files_expected`.\n\nRead the spec yourself for exact text. Quote it.\n\nFor each task: 5-10 concrete steps, exact file paths from tasks.json, exact verification commands from tasks.json. Coding-standard checks must include the standard limits (file ≤800, function ≤50, nesting ≤3) and no-broken-windows rules.\n\nWrite all 17 files, then report file count + first 3 paths confirmed. Do NOT modify tasks.json, context.md, or plan.md. Do NOT implement any code.","agentId":"a163eb6f432e922f5","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All 17 task brief files written successfully.\n\n**Files written: 17**\n\nFirst 3 paths confirmed:\n- `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T39.md`\n- `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T40.md`\n- `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T41.md`\n\nAll 17 files exist (T39, T40, T41, T42, T43, T44, T45, T46, R47, T48, T49, T50, T51, T52, T53, R54, R55) under `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/`.\n\nEach brief follows the prior-agent template (Goal, Spec excerpts that drive this task, Inputs, Steps with 5-10 concrete steps, Files expected, Verification with exact commands from tasks.json, Coding-standard checks citing the ≤800-line/≤50-line/≤3-nesting/≤10-cyclomatic limits and no-broken-windows rules, Definition of done checklist).\n\nKey task-specific points encoded as required:\n- **T39**: proposal-only model, `consolidation_jobs` + `consolidation_proposals` tables, bounded queue drops with warn log.\n- **T40**: synchronous deterministic episode template only, ≤5ms hot-path budget, LLM-forbid guard.\n- **T41**: deterministic scanners (duplicates, supersession, demotion, stale-marker, refresh) all routing through T39 proposals; `stale_marker.rs` + `refresh.rs` ship in this task per spec bullets.\n- **T42**: four LLM jobs with mode guard forbidding synchronous mode; malformed responses leave memory unchanged and emit `ConsolidationFailed` event.\n- **T43**: SHA-256 prompt/response hashes, per-kind queue bounds, budget catalog with fallback strategies, and the architecture doc at `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-consolidation-llm-budgets.md`.\n- **T44**: scope-gated manual review queue for repo + organization.\n- **T45**: replay-safe execution, reversal, cached-provenance replay (live LLM never called).\n- **T46**: integration tests covering proposals, apply/reject, replay, queue bounds, failure events.\n- **R47**: review with required headings (`## Spec alignment`, `## Coding-standard alignment`, `## Proposal discipline`, `## LLM budget evidence`, `## Findings`, `## Verdict`).\n- **T48**: eight-state output model verbatim, file/symbol/doc/test existence, `verification_jobs` table.\n- **T49**: exact-span content hashing, encoding-flip distinguished from content change, no whole-file reads.\n- **T50**: `ScopeFilter` enforced at store boundary, negative tests for all four scopes, `MemoryScopeFiltered` event.\n- **T51**: time-bound expiry, incremental verification narrowed to changed files/symbols, bounded work budget, snapshot-id advance on success.\n- **T52**: `SurfacingPipeline::partition` enforcing trusted vs advisory split, exhaustive `classify` match, ranker hard precondition excluding non-`Verified`.\n- **T53**: integration tests proving stale never trusted + scope leakage blocked across all eight states and all four scopes.\n- **R54**: review with required headings (`## Spec alignment`, `## Coding-standard alignment`, `## Stale-label discipline`, `## Scope-leak evidence`, `## Findings`, `## Verdict`).\n- **R55**: cross-layer contract gate with required headings; ships new test file `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/contract_tests/memory_verification_retrieval.rs` per tasks.json `files_expected`.\n\nNo code was implemented; tasks.json, context.md, and plan.md were not modified."}],"totalDurationMs":848530,"totalTokens":121791,"totalToolUseCount":37,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1191,"cache_read_input_tokens":119194,"output_tokens":1405,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1191},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":1405,"cache_read_input_tokens":119194,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1191,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1191,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":13,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":4,"editFileCount":17,"linesAdded":1295,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}}}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/R05.log:11:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013SN72EXjZoKVV7PgLUBYLw","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Cognitive Workspace Fork Plan\n2\t\n3\t**Date:** 2026-05-16\n4\t**Status:** Proposed\n5\t**Audience:** Lattice maintainers considering a fork or major successor architecture\n6\t**Source Context:** Lattice current code graph, workflow tools, structured memory, A-Mem, AgeMem, ViLoMem, and SRMA-style memory/reflection concepts\n7\t\n8\t## Goal\n9\t\n10\tBuild a mature assistant-facing workspace cognition system from Lattice's strongest primitives, without constraining the design to an incremental Lattice feature extension.\n11\t\n12\tThe target system should let coding agents understand a codebase, act on it, remember what happened, consolidate useful experience, verify whether memories still hold, and retrieve the right context with inspectable reasons.\n13\t\n14\tThe core product should be a local MCP server and daemon that exposes a unified engineering intelligence layer:\n15\t\n16\t- code graph\n17\t- document graph\n18\t- event log\n19\t- typed memory graph\n20\t- workflow engine\n21\t- consolidation engine\n22\t- verification engine\n23\t- explainable retrieval and ranking\n24\t- assistant usefulness metrics\n25\t\n26\tThe bar is not \"RAG over a repo.\" The bar is a trustworthy, auditable, long-running engineering memory system for agents.\n27\t\n28\t## Design Thesis\n29\t\n30\tThe correct abstraction is not memory attached to a code graph. The correct abstraction is a workspace cognition substrate with three first-class sources of truth:\n31\t\n32\t1. **Workspace graph:** files, symbols, docs, tests, dependencies, routes, schemas, build targets, runtime surfaces, and ownership boundaries.\n33\t2. **Event log:** every assistant action, tool call, context bundle, edit, diagnostic, test result, user correction, deployment step, and outcome.\n34\t3. **Memory graph:** durable typed claims over the workspace and event history, with evidence, freshness, contradiction, supersession, use history, and retrieval explanations.\n35\t\n36\tThese substrates should share identity, storage, and ranking primitives. A memory is not just text. It is a claim with evidence and validity conditions.\n37\t\n38\t## Research Concepts To Carry Forward\n39\t\n40\t### A-Mem\n41\t\n42\tA-Mem contributes the most directly useful memory model:\n43\t\n44\t- atomic memory notes\n45\t- generated context, tags, and keywords\n46\t- embeddings for semantic access\n47\t- explicit memory-to-memory links\n48\t- memory evolution as new notes arrive\n49\t\n50\tAdopt the structure and link evolution. Do not adopt silent uncontrolled mutation. In this system, memory evolution should produce auditable proposals or transactional updates with provenance.\n51\t\n52\t### AgeMem\n53\t\n54\tAgeMem is most useful as an operation and evaluation model:\n55\t\n56\t- separate short-term and long-term memory actions\n57\t- `ADD`, `UPDATE`, and `DELETE` for durable memory\n58\t- `RETRIEVE`, `SUMMARY`, and `FILTER` for working memory\n59\t- delayed usefulness signals for memory policy quality\n60\t\n61\tDo not start with reinforcement learning. First build deterministic event capture, retrieval telemetry, and offline usefulness scoring. Policy learning can only be credible after the substrate records enough real outcomes.\n62\t\n63\t### ViLoMem\n64\t\n65\tViLoMem's transferable idea is dual-stream memory and retrieval. For code agents, generalize that to multiple typed memory streams:\n66\t\n67\t- code topology\n68\t- workflow episodes\n69\t- failure patterns\n70\t- semantic repo claims\n71\t- architecture decisions\n72\t- user/team preferences\n73\t- docs and contract state\n74\t\n75\tEach stream should have its own ranking rules, freshness semantics, and consolidation policy.\n76\t\n77\t### SRMA-Style Reflection\n78\t\n79\tUse SRMA-like concepts as quality metrics:\n80\t\n81\t- reflection quality\n82\t- consolidation quality\n83\t- retention quality\n84\t- drift detection\n85\t- retrieval alignment\n86\t\n87\tDo not build opaque autonomous self-rewriting memory. Reflection should be inspectable, testable, and reversible.\n88\t\n89\t## Non-Negotiable Product Properties\n90\t\n91\t- Local-first by default.\n92\t- Deterministic identities for every file, symbol, doc section, event, and memory.\n93\t- Every durable memory has evidence.\n94\t- Every retrieved memory has an inclusion reason.\n95\t- Every stale or contradicted memory is surfaced as such, not hidden behind recency.\n96\t- Every workflow bundle is compact by default with deliberate expansion handles.\n97\t- Every background consolidation pass is recoverable, replayable, and observable.\n98\t- Every public MCP contract is documented and regression-tested.\n99\t- No silent broad workspace reads that bypass ignore rules or workspace boundaries.\n100\t- No unbounded graph traversal, payload growth, or event-log scans on hot paths.\n101\t\n102\t## System Architecture\n103\t\n104\t### 1. Ingestion Layer\n105\t\n106\tResponsibilities:\n107\t\n108\t- watch workspace changes\n109\t- parse code\n110\t- parse docs\n111\t- parse config and build files\n112\t- ingest test results and diagnostics\n113\t- capture assistant event traces\n114\t- normalize all inputs into stable records\n115\t\n116\tRequired inputs:\n117\t\n118\t- source files\n119\t- Markdown docs\n120\t- repo instruction files\n121\t- test files and test manifests\n122\t- package/build metadata\n123\t- compiler diagnostics\n124\t- test runner output\n125\t- MCP tool calls and responses\n126\t- assistant edits and generated diffs\n127\t- user corrections and accepted/rejected outcomes\n128\t\n129\tThe ingestion layer should never directly decide what context is important. It produces normalized facts and events.\n130\t\n131\t### 2. Workspace Graph\n132\t\n133\tThe workspace graph stores code, docs, tests, and operational surfaces.\n134\t\n135\tPrimary node families:\n136\t\n137\t- `File`\n138\t- `Directory`\n139\t- `Symbol`\n140\t- `Type`\n141\t- `Module`\n142\t- `Test`\n143\t- `Document`\n144\t- `Section`\n145\t- `ConfigKey`\n146\t- `Command`\n147\t- `Route`\n148\t- `Schema`\n149\t- `Package`\n150\t- `BuildTarget`\n151\t- `RuntimeSurface`\n152\t\n153\tPrimary edge families:\n154\t\n155\t- `contains`\n156\t- `imports`\n157\t- `calls`\n158\t- `implements`\n159\t- `extends`\n160\t- `type_ref`\n161\t- `tested_by`\n162\t- `documents`\n163\t- `mentions`\n164\t- `depends_on`\n165\t- `configured_by`\n166\t- `generated_by`\n167\t- `co_changed_with`\n168\t- `stale_against`\n169\t\n170\tThe graph must support exact identity lookup, bounded neighborhood traversal, impact queries, doc/code backlinks, and freshness queries.\n171\t\n172\t### 3. Event Log\n173\t\n174\tThe event log is append-only and replayable. It is the substrate that makes memory consolidation and usefulness measurement possible.\n175\t\n176\tRequired event types:\n177\t\n178\t- `AssistantTaskStarted`\n179\t- `ToolCalled`\n180\t- `ToolResult`\n181\t- `ContextBundleReturned`\n182\t- `MemoryRetrieved`\n183\t- `MemoryExpanded`\n184\t- `PlanCreated`\n185\t- `FileRead`\n186\t- `PatchApplied`\n187\t- `TestRunStarted`\n188\t- `TestRunCompleted`\n189\t- `DiagnosticObserved`\n190\t- `UserCorrection`\n191\t- `UserPreferenceObserved`\n192\t- `WorkflowSucceeded`\n193\t- `WorkflowFailed`\n194\t- `MemoryCreated`\n195\t- `MemoryUpdated`\n196\t- `MemoryInvalidated`\n197\t- `MemoryConsolidated`\n198\t\n199\tEvents should carry:\n200\t\n201\t- workspace id\n202\t- branch\n203\t- session id\n204\t- task id\n205\t- actor\n206\t- timestamp\n207\t- stable references to files, symbols, docs, events, and memories\n208\t- payload hash\n209\t- compact payload summary\n210\t- optional full payload location\n211\t\n212\tThe full event stream should not be injected into prompts. It exists for retrieval, audit, replay, metrics, and consolidation.\n213\t\n214\t### 4. Memory Graph\n215\t\n216\tMemory should be stored as typed claims and episodes, not loose notes.\n217\t\n218\tPrimary memory classes:\n219\t\n220\t- `Observation`\n221\t- `Decision`\n222\t- `Constraint`\n223\t- `Pattern`\n224\t- `AntiPattern`\n225\t- `WorkflowOutcome`\n226\t- `FailurePattern`\n227\t- `Procedure`\n228\t- `Preference`\n229\t- `ArchitectureInvariant`\n230\t- `DocsContract`\n231\t- `OpenQuestion`\n232\t- `CounterMemory` — a memory record that explicitly asserts a prior memory is wrong or no longer applicable. Distinct from a `contradicts` link: a link connects two existing memories; a CounterMemory is a first-class record authored by an agent or user that carries its own evidence, scope, and verification status and can itself be superseded or invalidated. Use CounterMemory when the counter-claim needs its own provenance and lifecycle, not just an edge annotation.\n233\t\n234\tEach memory record requires:\n235\t\n236\t- stable id\n237\t- content\n238\t- memory class\n239\t- assertion type\n240\t- scope: session, branch, repo, user, organization\n241\t- verification status\n242\t- confidence and confidence reason\n243\t- freshness policy\n244\t- validity conditions\n245\t- invalidation triggers\n246\t- provenance events\n247\t- evidence references\n248\t- linked files\n249\t- linked symbols\n250\t- linked docs\n251\t- linked tests\n252\t- linked memories\n253\t- contradiction links\n254\t- supersession links\n255\t- access history\n256\t- usefulness scores\n257\t- last verified state\n258\t\n259\tMemory links should be first-class records:\n260\t\n261\t- source memory\n262\t- target memory or graph node\n263\t- link type\n264\t- strength\n265\t- reason\n266\t- evidence event\n267\t- created by\n268\t- created at\n269\t- verification status\n270\t\n271\tRequired link types:\n272\t\n273\t- `supports`\n274\t- `contradicts`\n275\t- `supersedes`\n276\t- `refines`\n277\t- `generalizes`\n278\t- `specializes`\n279\t- `co_occurs_with`\n280\t- `derived_from`\n281\t- `applies_to`\n282\t- `validated_by`\n283\t- `invalidated_by`\n284\t\n285\t### 5. Working Memory\n286\t\n287\tWorking memory is per-task and short-lived. It should be explicit rather than implicit prompt accumulation.\n288\t\n289\tWorking memory contains:\n290\t\n291\t- task statement\n292\t- interpreted intent\n293\t- active files and symbols\n294\t- active hypotheses\n295\t- active failures\n296\t- current plan\n297\t- selected memories\n298\t- excluded memories and reasons\n299\t- budget decisions\n300\t- unresolved questions\n301\t- verification status\n302\t\n303\tRequired operations:\n304\t\n305\t- `retrieve`\n306\t- `summarize`\n307\t- `filter`\n308\t- `pin`\n309\t- `evict`\n310\t- `expand`\n311\t- `compress`\n312\t- `checkpoint`\n313\t\n314\tWorking memory is not durable by itself. Durable value is extracted through event-backed consolidation.\n315\t\n316\t### 6. Consolidation Engine\n317\t\n318\tThe consolidation engine turns event traces into better memory.\n319\t\n320\tConsolidation jobs:\n321\t\n322\t- create episode summaries from completed tasks\n323\t- promote repeated successful workflow traces into procedures\n324\t- promote recurring failures into failure patterns\n325\t- promote verified implementation facts into semantic repo memory\n326\t- detect duplicate memories\n327\t- detect contradictions\n328\t- detect supersession candidates\n329\t- demote unused or low-value memories\n330\t- mark stale memories after graph changes\n331\t- refresh memories whose evidence still matches current code\n332\t- propose docs updates when memory and docs diverge\n333\t\n334\tConsolidation modes:\n335\t\n336\t- synchronous post-task consolidation for small task traces\n337\t- background scheduled consolidation for larger histories\n338\t- manual review mode for high-impact repo or organization memories\n339\t- replay mode for rebuilding memory state from the event log\n340\t\n341\tNo consolidation job may silently rewrite high-scope memory without preserving provenance and prior state.\n342\t\n343\tLLM-driven consolidation:\n344\t\n345\tSeveral consolidation jobs (episode summary generation, procedure extraction, contradiction detection, failure-pattern extraction) cannot be implemented deterministically and require LLM inference. These jobs must follow additional constraints:\n346\t\n347\t- each LLM-driven job produces a proposal record, not a direct write; proposals require explicit apply or reject before altering durable memory\n348\t- failed or malformed LLM responses must leave the prior memory state unchanged and emit a consolidation failure event\n349\t- LLM-driven jobs run only in background or manual review modes, never on the synchronous post-task hot path\n350\t- each job records its model, prompt hash, and response hash as part of the proposal provenance so outputs are auditable and reproducible\n351\t- consolidation queue depth must be bounded; when the queue is full, new jobs are dropped with a log warning rather than stalling the daemon\n352\t- cost and latency budgets for LLM consolidation should be documented per job type before Phase 6 begins; jobs exceeding budget must fall back to deterministic approximations or skip with a stale flag\n353\t\n354\t### 7. Retrieval Engine\n355\t\n356\tRetrieval must be task-aware and explainable.\n357\t\n358\tPipeline:\n359\t\n360\t1. Parse user task and classify intent.\n361\t2. Extract literal anchors: paths, symbols, errors, commands, APIs, config keys.\n362\t3. Resolve anchors into graph identities.\n363\t4. Retrieve graph candidates.\n364\t5. Retrieve memory candidates from typed streams.\n365\t6. Retrieve relevant event episodes.\n366\t7. Expand through bounded graph and memory neighborhoods.\n367\t8. Score candidates.\n368\t9. Deduplicate and compress.\n369\t10. Return a compact bundle with inclusion reasons and expansion handles.\n370\t\n371\tCandidate sources:\n372\t\n373\t- exact path/symbol lookup\n374\t- code graph traversal\n375\t- doc backlinks/outgoing links\n376\t- FTS\n377\t- embeddings\n378\t- event similarity\n379\t- memory links\n380\t- workflow similarity\n381\t- recent active working memory\n382\t\n383\tRanking signals:\n384\t\n385\t- task-type compatibility\n386\t- graph proximity to anchors\n387\t- exact identifier match\n388\t- semantic similarity\n389\t- verification status\n390\t- freshness\n391\t- scope\n392\t- evidence strength\n393\t- contradiction/supersession state\n394\t- past usefulness\n395\t- recent successful reuse\n396\t- user preference compatibility\n397\t- token cost\n398\t\n399\tThe ranker should expose scores and reasons in diagnostic mode. Compact mode should include only enough explanation for an assistant to trust the result.\n400\t\n401\t### 8. Verification Engine\n402\t\n403\tThe verification engine keeps memory honest.\n404\t\n405\tVerification checks:\n406\t\n407\t- linked files still exist\n408\t- linked symbols still exist\n409\t- cited docs still exist\n410\t- linked tests still exist\n411\t- evidence text still matches when exact spans were captured\n412\t- implementation still matches memory claim where deterministic checks are possible\n413\t- contradicted/superseded states remain coherent\n414\t- branch-scoped memory is not leaking into unrelated branches\n415\t- time-bound memory has expired\n416\t\n417\tVerification outputs:\n418\t\n419\t- `verified`\n420\t- `unverified`\n421\t- `in_review`\n422\t- `stale`\n423\t- `contradicted`\n424\t- `superseded`\n425\t- `expired`\n426\t- `invalidated`\n427\t\n428\tVerification must be incremental and tied to workspace changes. Large repos cannot tolerate full rescans for every memory.\n429\t\n430\t### 9. MCP Surface\n431\t\n432\tThe MCP contract should expose high-level assistant workflows rather than forcing clients to compose low-level graph calls.\n433\t\n434\tRequired workflow tools:\n435\t\n436\t- `get_context_capsule`\n437\t- `prepare_change`\n438\t- `plan_edit`\n439\t- `trace_scenario`\n440\t- `diagnose_failure`\n441\t- `find_relevant_tests`\n442\t- `impact_from_diff`\n443\t- `get_docs_capsule`\n444\t- `get_backlinks`\n445\t- `expand_context`\n446\t- `get_working_set_context`\n447\t- `get_repo_playbook`\n448\t- `search_symbols`\n449\t- `search_memory`\n450\t- `record_workflow_outcome`\n451\t\n452\tNew or redesigned memory tools:\n453\t\n454\t- `get_task_memory`\n455\t- `save_memory`\n456\t- `propose_memory_evolution`\n457\t- `apply_memory_evolution`\n458\t- `verify_memory`\n459\t- `explain_memory`\n460\t- `list_memory_conflicts`\n461\t- `consolidate_session`\n462\t- `get_memory_metrics`\n463\t- `get_event_trace`\n464\t\n465\tTool surface discipline: ten new memory tools is a meaningful cognitive load for clients. Before shipping Phase 8, audit whether `propose_memory_evolution` + `apply_memory_evolution` can collapse into a single tool with an `action` parameter, and whether `verify_memory` + `explain_memory` can be unified. The goal is the smallest surface that covers all assistant workflows. Consolidate before stabilizing the MCP contract.\n466\t\n467\tEvery tool response should support:\n468\t\n469\t- compact rendering\n470\t- full structured JSON\n471\t- context handles\n472\t- stable expansion targets\n473\t- budget controls\n474\t- diagnostic explanations where useful\n475\t\n476\t### 10. Human Review Surface\n477\t\n478\tAgents are the primary client, but durable memory needs human inspectability.\n479\t\n480\tRequired operator views:\n481\t\n482\t- memory inbox\n483\t- proposed promotions\n484\t- proposed contradictions\n485\t- stale memory list\n486\t- memory evidence view\n487\t- event trace view\n488\t- retrieval explanation view\n489\t- usefulness metrics\n490\t- workspace graph health\n491\t- indexing health\n492\t- consolidation queue\n493\t\n494\tThis can be a VS Code extension panel or local web UI. It should be a review and trust surface, not the center of the product.\n495\t\n496\t## Storage Design\n497\t\n498\tUse SQLite as the durable local store unless proven insufficient. Split logical tables cleanly.\n499\t\n500\tCore tables:\n501\t\n502\t- `workspaces`\n503\t- `files`\n504\t- `symbols`\n505\t- `documents`\n506\t- `sections`\n507\t- `tests`\n508\t- `graph_edges`\n509\t- `events`\n510\t- `event_payloads`\n511\t- `memories`\n512\t- `memory_links`\n513\t- `memory_evidence`\n514\t- `memory_accesses`\n515\t- `memory_scores`\n516\t- `working_memory_checkpoints`\n517\t- `context_handles`\n518\t- `consolidation_jobs`\n519\t- `verification_jobs`\n520\t\n521\tIndexes:\n522\t\n523\t- graph identity indexes\n524\t- file path indexes\n525\t- symbol name/path indexes\n526\t- doc section indexes\n527\t- event task/session indexes\n528\t- memory scope/type/status indexes\n529\t- memory link source/target indexes\n530\t- freshness and verification indexes\n531\t- FTS over code summary, docs, memory, and event summaries\n532\t- vector indexes over symbols, doc sections, memories, and event episodes\n533\t\n534\tThe event log should be append-only. Derived graph and memory state can be rebuilt from source files and events.\n535\t\n536\tEvent log compaction: append-only storage will grow unboundedly for long-running repos. The storage design must include a compaction snapshot strategy: at configurable intervals, write a full-state snapshot of graph and memory to a separate file, then truncate the event log to events after the snapshot timestamp. The snapshot format must be versioned and independently readable so the daemon can bootstrap from a snapshot without replaying the full history. Compaction must be a daemon-managed background operation, not a user-initiated manual step.\n537\t\n538\t## Fork Strategy\n539\t\n540\tThis should be a fork only if the implementation needs to break major internal assumptions. Preserve useful Lattice assets.\n541\t\n542\tCarry forward:\n543\t\n544\t- Rust daemon structure\n545\t- MCP handling experience\n546\t- workspace boundary protections\n547\t- existing workflow tool vocabulary\n548\t- graph parsing and indexing foundation\n549\t- context handles\n550\t- compact/full render modes\n551\t- structured memory groundwork\n552\t- test discipline around daemon behavior\n553\t- VS Code extension integration pattern\n554\t\n555\tReplace or redesign:\n556\t\n557\t- memory retrieval as keyword search\n558\t- memory as mostly isolated rows\n559\t- workflow metrics as secondary side data\n560\t- event capture as implicit logs instead of a first-class substrate\n561\t- ranking paths that cannot explain why context was included\n562\t- any storage path that cannot be replayed or verified\n563\t\n564\t## Implementation Plan\n565\t\n566\t### Phase 0: Fork Foundation\n567\t\n568\tDeliverables:\n569\t\n570\t- new repo or long-lived branch with clear successor name\n571\t- architecture docs copied and revised\n572\t- crate/module boundary plan\n573\t- storage migration policy\n574\t- compatibility policy for existing MCP tools\n575\t- baseline benchmark suite from current Lattice workflows\n576\t\n577\tFork decision gate:\n578\t\n579\tPhase 0 ends with an explicit fork/extend decision before any structural code changes begin. Fork if any of the following are true:\n580\t\n581\t- the memory graph schema changes require breaking the existing `memories` table in a way that cannot be migrated without a full rebuild\n582\t- the identity model requires changing the primary key or graph edge representation in a way that invalidates existing indexed data\n583\t- the event log requires a new storage file or WAL mode incompatible with the current daemon's SQLite usage\n584\t- two or more of the above apply and sharing a codebase would require sustained compatibility shims across all phases\n585\t\n586\tIf none of those conditions hold, extend in place on a long-lived branch and skip the repo split. Document the decision and the evidence for it before Phase 1 begins.\n587\t\n588\tVerification:\n589\t\n590\t- current Lattice daemon tests pass in the fork before major changes\n591\t- current MCP workflow fixtures run unchanged or with documented compatibility shims\n592\t\n593\t### Phase 1: Unified Identity Model\n594\t\n595\tIdentity must precede the event log. An event that references a file, symbol, or doc by unstable name cannot be reliably replayed or used for consolidation. This phase establishes the identity substrate that all subsequent phases depend on.\n596\t\n597\tDeliverables:\n598\t\n599\t- stable ids for files, symbols, docs, sections, events, memories, and context handles\n600\t- identity resolver for paths, symbols, headings, tests, and event refs\n601\t- explicit identity serialization in MCP payloads\n602\t- compatibility support for legacy name-based references\n603\t- tests for duplicate symbol names, renamed files, moved sections, and branch changes\n604\t\n605\tDefinition of done:\n606\t\n607\t- every workflow output can be expanded through a stable identity\n608\t- ambiguous names return ambiguity diagnostics instead of arbitrary matches\n609\t- identity resolution adds no more than 2ms P99 to hot-path tool calls\n610\t\n611\t### Phase 2: Event Log Substrate\n612\t\n613\tDeliverables:\n614\t\n615\t- event model\n616\t- append-only SQLite event tables\n617\t- event writer API\n618\t- event reader/query API\n619\t- event payload hashing and optional payload spillover\n620\t- MCP/tool-call event capture using stable identities from Phase 1\n621\t- workflow task/session correlation\n622\t- tests for ordering, replay, corruption handling, and workspace scoping\n623\t\n624\tDefinition of done:\n625\t\n626\t- every high-level MCP workflow records task, retrieval, response, and outcome events\n627\t- event log can replay enough data to reconstruct workflow history\n628\t- failure paths emit useful events without hiding errors\n629\t- event write adds no more than 5ms P99 to hot-path tool calls (`prepare_change`, `get_context_capsule`); large payloads spill to a side table rather than blocking the writer\n630\t\n631\t### Phase 3: Memory Graph Storage\n632\t\n633\tDeliverables:\n634\t\n635\t- redesigned memory schema\n636\t- first-class memory links\n637\t- first-class memory evidence\n638\t- memory access/use history\n639\t- contradiction and supersession as graph edges\n640\t- memory stream/type taxonomy\n641\t- migration/import path from existing Lattice memory rows\n642\t- tests for memory creation, linking, contradiction, supersession, stale state, and replay\n643\t\n644\tDefinition of done:\n645\t\n646\t- memory can represent atomic notes, claims, workflow outcomes, procedures, preferences, and counter-memory\n647\t- every memory can explain its evidence and relationship state\n648\t\n649\t### Phase 4: Retrieval V1\n650\t\n651\tDeliverables:\n652\t\n653\t- task intent classifier\n654\t- anchor extractor and resolver\n655\t- hybrid candidate retrieval from graph, docs, memories, and events\n656\t- scoring model with graph proximity, semantic similarity, freshness, scope, verification, and usefulness\n657\t- compact response shaper\n658\t- diagnostic response mode with ranking reasons\n659\t- benchmark suite for realistic assistant tasks\n660\t\n661\tScope constraint: Phase 4 retrieval must not depend on the formal working memory state model defined in Phase 5. Retrieval returns bundles into the calling context; working memory formalization is layered on top in Phase 5. Design the retrieval output schema to be forward-compatible with working memory checkpoints without requiring a Phase 4 rewrite.\n662\t\n663\tDefinition of done:\n664\t\n665\t- memory retrieval no longer depends on keyword search as the primary path\n666\t- workflow tools return memory with inclusion reasons and stable expansion handles\n667\t- irrelevant memory rate is measured and regressed\n668\t\n669\t### Phase 5: Working Memory\n670\t\n671\tDeliverables:\n672\t\n673\t- explicit per-task working memory state\n674\t- operations for retrieve, summarize, filter, pin, evict, expand, compress, and checkpoint\n675\t- MCP surface for inspecting current working memory\n676\t- automatic event capture for included and excluded context\n677\t- tests for token budgets, pinned context, eviction, and checkpoint restore\n678\t\n679\tDefinition of done:\n680\t\n681\t- workflow tools can show what context is active, why it is active, and what was intentionally excluded\n682\t- context management is no longer just prompt accumulation\n683\t\n684\t### Phase 6: Consolidation Engine\n685\t\n686\tDeliverables:\n687\t\n688\t- session consolidation jobs\n689\t- episode summary generation\n690\t- memory promotion rules\n691\t- duplicate detection\n692\t- contradiction detection\n693\t- supersession proposal\n694\t- procedure extraction from repeated successful workflows\n695\t- failure-pattern extraction from repeated diagnostics\n696\t- manual review queue for high-scope memory changes\n697\t- replay-safe job execution\n698\t\n699\tDefinition of done:\n700\t\n701\t- completed tasks produce useful episode memories\n702\t- repeated successful traces produce procedural memories\n703\t- consolidation is auditable, reversible, and test-covered\n704\t\n705\t### Phase 7: Verification And Freshness\n706\t\n707\tDeliverables:\n708\t\n709\t- incremental memory verification against graph changes\n710\t- file/symbol/doc/test existence checks\n711\t- exact-span evidence validation where available\n712\t- branch and workspace scope enforcement\n713\t- time-bound expiry\n714\t- stale memory surfacing in workflow bundles\n715\t- verification jobs and operator diagnostics\n716\t\n717\tDefinition of done:\n718\t\n719\t- stale or contradicted memory cannot appear as normal trusted guidance\n720\t- verification results are explainable and queryable\n721\t\n722\t### Phase 8: Workflow Engine V2\n723\t\n724\tDeliverables:\n725\t\n726\t- redesigned versions of context, change planning, scenario tracing, failure diagnosis, docs retrieval, test selection, impact analysis, and playbook generation\n727\t- workflow composition over graph, event, memory, and working-memory substrates\n728\t- one-call edit-planning bundles with code, docs, tests, memory, risks, and verification commands\n729\t- workflow outcome recording integrated by default\n730\t\n731\tDefinition of done:\n732\t\n733\t- common coding tasks require fewer manual discovery calls\n734\t- workflows produce enough context to implement and verify changes without broad file dumping\n735\t\n736\t### Phase 9: Metrics And Evaluation\n737\t\n738\tDeliverables:\n739\t\n740\t- retrieval relevance metrics\n741\t- memory usefulness metrics\n742\t- context token efficiency metrics\n743\t- stale/contradicted memory surfacing metrics\n744\t- test-pass-after-plan metrics\n745\t- repeated-failure reduction metrics\n746\t- benchmark tasks with golden expected anchors\n747\t- regression dashboard or CLI report\n748\t\n749\tRequired metrics:\n750\t\n751\t- tool calls per successful task\n752\t- irrelevant files opened per task\n753\t- relevant anchor recall\n754\t- memory inclusion precision\n755\t- memory later-used rate\n756\t- stale memory surfaced rate\n757\t- contradiction missed rate\n758\t- tests recommended versus tests needed\n759\t- workflow success after first plan\n760\t\n761\tDefinition of done:\n762\t\n763\t- changes to retrieval, memory, and workflow behavior can be evaluated without relying on anecdote\n764\t\n765\t### Phase 10: Human Review And Extension UX\n766\t\n767\tDeliverables:\n768\t\n769\t- VS Code or local web review UI\n770\t- memory inbox\n771\t- promotion queue\n772\t- contradiction queue\n773\t- stale memory view\n774\t- event trace view\n775\t- retrieval explanation view\n776\t- consolidation job health\n777\t- indexing health\n778\t- one-click accept/reject for memory evolution proposals\n779\t\n780\tDefinition of done:\n781\t\n782\t- operators can understand and correct durable memory without reading raw SQLite rows\n783\t\n784\t### Phase 11: Hardening\n785\t\n786\tDeliverables:\n787\t\n788\t- large-repo performance tests\n789\t- concurrency tests\n790\t- recovery tests\n791\t- corrupted-event handling\n792\t- partial-index handling\n793\t- workspace-boundary tests\n794\t- migration tests\n795\t- MCP schema compatibility tests\n796\t- documentation for setup, operations, memory semantics, and workflow behavior\n797\t\n798\tDefinition of done:\n799\t\n800\t- the system is production-grade for repeated agent use, not only successful on a toy repo\n801\t\n802\t## MCP Tool Contract Principles\n803\t\n804\tEvery assistant-facing tool should return:\n805\t\n806\t- `overview`\n807\t- ranked pivots\n808\t- relevant context\n809\t- memory highlights\n810\t- event episodes where relevant\n811\t- suggested next expansion\n812\t- stable handles\n813\t- risks or uncertainty\n814\t- compact/full render choice\n815\t- structured payload\n816\t\n817\tEvery memory-bearing response should identify:\n818\t\n819\t- why the memory was included\n820\t- evidence strength\n821\t- verification status\n822\t- freshness status\n823\t- contradiction/supersession state\n824\t- scope\n825\t- expansion target\n826\t\n827\tEvery workflow tool should record:\n828\t\n829\t- inputs\n830\t- resolved anchors\n831\t- selected candidates\n832\t- excluded high-scoring candidates where useful\n833\t- response summary\n834\t- downstream use events\n835\t\n836\t## Documentation Requirements\n837\t\n838\tRequired docs:\n839\t\n840\t- successor architecture overview\n841\t- MCP contract reference\n842\t- memory model reference\n843\t- event log design\n844\t- consolidation design\n845\t- retrieval/ranking design\n846\t- verification/freshness design\n847\t- operator guide\n848\t- migration guide from Lattice\n849\t- benchmark/evaluation guide\n850\t- extension review UI guide\n851\t\n852\tDocs must be updated in the same change as public behavior changes.\n853\t\n854\t## Testing Requirements\n855\t\n856\tRequired test families:\n857\t\n858\t- storage migration tests\n859\t- event append/replay tests\n860\t- graph identity tests\n861\t- parser tests\n862\t- memory link tests\n863\t- contradiction/supersession tests\n864\t- stale verification tests\n865\t- retrieval ranking golden tests\n866\t- workflow golden tests\n867\t- MCP schema tests\n868\t- compact/full render tests\n869\t- concurrency tests\n870\t- large fixture performance tests\n871\t- extension compile/UI smoke tests\n872\t\n873\tNo phase is complete until its failure paths are tested.\n874\t\n875\t## Measurable Success Criteria\n876\t\n877\tThe fork should beat current Lattice on:\n878\t\n879\t- lower tool calls per coding task\n880\t- lower irrelevant context inclusion\n881\t- higher relevant file/symbol recall\n882\t- higher memory reuse precision\n883\t- lower stale memory surfacing\n884\t- better repeated-failure avoidance\n885\t- better test recommendation accuracy\n886\t- better user correction retention\n887\t- faster diagnosis from failures\n888\t- more explainable context selection\n889\t\n890\tInitial targets:\n891\t\n892\t- 30 percent fewer discovery tool calls on benchmark tasks\n893\t- 40 percent fewer irrelevant file reads\n894\t- 80 percent memory inclusion precision on curated memory benchmarks\n895\t- zero trusted display of known contradicted memory\n896\t- zero trusted display of known stale memory without stale label\n897\t- 90 percent correct relevant-test recommendation on curated tasks\n898\t\n899\t## Risks\n900\t\n901\t### Opaque Memory Mutation\n902\t\n903\tRisk: automatic consolidation corrupts durable repo knowledge.\n904\t\n905\tControl: event-backed proposals, reversible updates, provenance, review queue for high-scope changes, and replay.\n906\t\n907\t### Ranking Complexity\n908\t\n909\tRisk: ranking becomes hard to reason about.\n910\t\n911\tControl: keep ranker feature-based and inspectable before any learned policy. Expose diagnostic scores.\n912\t\n913\t### Event Log Growth\n914\t\n915\tRisk: event storage becomes too large or slow.\n916\t\n917\tControl: append compact summaries on hot paths, spill large payloads, index by task/session/workspace, and support compaction snapshots.\n918\t\n919\t### Stale Memory Leakage\n920\t\n921\tRisk: agents trust outdated memory.\n922\t\n923\tControl: freshness indexes, graph-change-triggered verification, stale labels in all memory surfaces, and tests that stale memory cannot rank as trusted.\n924\t\n925\t### Scope Leakage\n926\t\n927\tRisk: user/org/branch memories leak into wrong workspaces.\n928\t\n929\tControl: scope-aware queries, enforced filters in store APIs, and negative tests.\n930\t\n931\t### Overfitting To Current Repo\n932\t\n933\tRisk: benchmarks pass only for Lattice-like Rust/TypeScript repos.\n934\t\n935\tControl: fixture repos across languages, repo sizes, and documentation styles.\n936\t\n937\t## First Implementation Slice\n938\t\n939\tThe first real slice should be event log plus memory graph, not UI.\n940\t\n941\tImplement in this order:\n942\t\n943\t1. Event model and append-only store.\n944\t2. MCP workflow event capture.\n945\t3. Memory graph schema and migration/import from existing rows.\n946\t4. Memory access/use history.\n947\t5. Retrieval V1 with graph-aware memory ranking.\n948\t6. Compact memory inclusion reasons in existing workflow tools.\n949\t7. Session consolidation into episode memories.\n950\t8. Verification and stale surfacing tests.\n951\t\n952\tThis slice creates the substrate that all later intelligence depends on. Building UI or learned policy before this substrate would create debt instead of capability.\n953\t\n954\t## Final Target\n955\t\n956\tThe final system should make a coding agent feel like it is operating in a workspace that remembers accurately:\n957\t\n958\t- what the repo contains\n959\t- why the repo is shaped that way\n960\t- what was tried before\n961\t- what failed before\n962\t- what tests prove behavior\n963\t- what memories are trustworthy\n964\t- what memories are stale\n965\t- what context matters now\n966\t- what should be done next\n967\t\n968\tThat is the measurable leap over a code graph plus ad hoc memory store.\n969\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4cb46183-2e7c-4dbc-b888-4ae03f7bba08","uuid":"d5e243ea-3d28-4697-91ea-bce60d5527b4","timestamp":"2026-05-17T03:27:41.328Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md","content":"# Cognitive Workspace Fork Plan\n\n**Date:** 2026-05-16\n**Status:** Proposed\n**Audience:** Lattice maintainers considering a fork or major successor architecture\n**Source Context:** Lattice current code graph, workflow tools, structured memory, A-Mem, AgeMem, ViLoMem, and SRMA-style memory/reflection concepts\n\n## Goal\n\nBuild a mature assistant-facing workspace cognition system from Lattice's strongest primitives, without constraining the design to an incremental Lattice feature extension.\n\nThe target system should let coding agents understand a codebase, act on it, remember what happened, consolidate useful experience, verify whether memories still hold, and retrieve the right context with inspectable reasons.\n\nThe core product should be a local MCP server and daemon that exposes a unified engineering intelligence layer:\n\n- code graph\n- document graph\n- event log\n- typed memory graph\n- workflow engine\n- consolidation engine\n- verification engine\n- explainable retrieval and ranking\n- assistant usefulness metrics\n\nThe bar is not \"RAG over a repo.\" The bar is a trustworthy, auditable, long-running engineering memory system for agents.\n\n## Design Thesis\n\nThe correct abstraction is not memory attached to a code graph. The correct abstraction is a workspace cognition substrate with three first-class sources of truth:\n\n1. **Workspace graph:** files, symbols, docs, tests, dependencies, routes, schemas, build targets, runtime surfaces, and ownership boundaries.\n2. **Event log:** every assistant action, tool call, context bundle, edit, diagnostic, test result, user correction, deployment step, and outcome.\n3. **Memory graph:** durable typed claims over the workspace and event history, with evidence, freshness, contradiction, supersession, use history, and retrieval explanations.\n\nThese substrates should share identity, storage, and ranking primitives. A memory is not just text. It is a claim with evidence and validity conditions.\n\n## Research Concepts To Carry Forward\n\n### A-Mem\n\nA-Mem contributes the most directly useful memory model:\n\n- atomic memory notes\n- generated context, tags, and keywords\n- embeddings for semantic access\n- explicit memory-to-memory links\n- memory evolution as new notes arrive\n\nAdopt the structure and link evolution. Do not adopt silent uncontrolled mutation. In this system, memory evolution should produce auditable proposals or transactional updates with provenance.\n\n### AgeMem\n\nAgeMem is most useful as an operation and evaluation model:\n\n- separate short-term and long-term memory actions\n- `ADD`, `UPDATE`, and `DELETE` for durable memory\n- `RETRIEVE`, `SUMMARY`, and `FILTER` for working memory\n- delayed usefulness signals for memory policy quality\n\nDo not start with reinforcement learning. First build deterministic event capture, retrieval telemetry, and offline usefulness scoring. Policy learning can only be credible after the substrate records enough real outcomes.\n\n### ViLoMem\n\nViLoMem's transferable idea is dual-stream memory and retrieval. For code agents, generalize that to multiple typed memory streams:\n\n- code topology\n- workflow episodes\n- failure patterns\n- semantic repo claims\n- architecture decisions\n- user/team preferences\n- docs and contract state\n\nEach stream should have its own ranking rules, freshness semantics, and consolidation policy.\n\n### SRMA-Style Reflection\n\nUse SRMA-like concepts as quality metrics:\n\n- reflection quality\n- consolidation quality\n- retention quality\n- drift detection\n- retrieval alignment\n\nDo not build opaque autonomous self-rewriting memory. Reflection should be inspectable, testable, and reversible.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Product Properties\n\n- Local-first by default.\n- Deterministic identities for every file, symbol, doc section, event, and memory.\n- Every durable memory has evidence.\n- Every retrieved memory has an inclusion reason.\n- Every stale or contradicted memory is surfaced as such, not hidden behind recency.\n- Every workflow bundle is compact by default with deliberate expansion handles.\n- Every background consolidation pass is recoverable, replayable, and observable.\n- Every public MCP contract is documented and regression-tested.\n- No silent broad workspace reads that bypass ignore rules or workspace boundaries.\n- No unbounded graph traversal, payload growth, or event-log scans on hot paths.\n\n## System Architecture\n\n### 1. Ingestion Layer\n\nResponsibilities:\n\n- watch workspace changes\n- parse code\n- parse docs\n- parse config and build files\n- ingest test results and diagnostics\n- capture assistant event traces\n- normalize all inputs into stable records\n\nRequired inputs:\n\n- source files\n- Markdown docs\n- repo instruction files\n- test files and test manifests\n- package/build metadata\n- compiler diagnostics\n- test runner output\n- MCP tool calls and responses\n- assistant edits and generated diffs\n- user corrections and accepted/rejected outcomes\n\nThe ingestion layer should never directly decide what context is important. It produces normalized facts and events.\n\n### 2. Workspace Graph\n\nThe workspace graph stores code, docs, tests, and operational surfaces.\n\nPrimary node families:\n\n- `File`\n- `Directory`\n- `Symbol`\n- `Type`\n- `Module`\n- `Test`\n- `Document`\n- `Section`\n- `ConfigKey`\n- `Command`\n- `Route`\n- `Schema`\n- `Package`\n- `BuildTarget`\n- `RuntimeSurface`\n\nPrimary edge families:\n\n- `contains`\n- `imports`\n- `calls`\n- `implements`\n- `extends`\n- `type_ref`\n- `tested_by`\n- `documents`\n- `mentions`\n- `depends_on`\n- `configured_by`\n- `generated_by`\n- `co_changed_with`\n- `stale_against`\n\nThe graph must support exact identity lookup, bounded neighborhood traversal, impact queries, doc/code backlinks, and freshness queries.\n\n### 3. Event Log\n\nThe event log is append-only and replayable. It is the substrate that makes memory consolidation and usefulness measurement possible.\n\nRequired event types:\n\n- `AssistantTaskStarted`\n- `ToolCalled`\n- `ToolResult`\n- `ContextBundleReturned`\n- `MemoryRetrieved`\n- `MemoryExpanded`\n- `PlanCreated`\n- `FileRead`\n- `PatchApplied`\n- `TestRunStarted`\n- `TestRunCompleted`\n- `DiagnosticObserved`\n- `UserCorrection`\n- `UserPreferenceObserved`\n- `WorkflowSucceeded`\n- `WorkflowFailed`\n- `MemoryCreated`\n- `MemoryUpdated`\n- `MemoryInvalidated`\n- `MemoryConsolidated`\n\nEvents should carry:\n\n- workspace id\n- branch\n- session id\n- task id\n- actor\n- timestamp\n- stable references to files, symbols, docs, events, and memories\n- payload hash\n- compact payload summary\n- optional full payload location\n\nThe full event stream should not be injected into prompts. It exists for retrieval, audit, replay, metrics, and consolidation.\n\n### 4. Memory Graph\n\nMemory should be stored as typed claims and episodes, not loose notes.\n\nPrimary memory classes:\n\n- `Observation`\n- `Decision`\n- `Constraint`\n- `Pattern`\n- `AntiPattern`\n- `WorkflowOutcome`\n- `FailurePattern`\n- `Procedure`\n- `Preference`\n- `ArchitectureInvariant`\n- `DocsContract`\n- `OpenQuestion`\n- `CounterMemory` — a memory record that explicitly asserts a prior memory is wrong or no longer applicable. Distinct from a `contradicts` link: a link connects two existing memories; a CounterMemory is a first-class record authored by an agent or user that carries its own evidence, scope, and verification status and can itself be superseded or invalidated. Use CounterMemory when the counter-claim needs its own provenance and lifecycle, not just an edge annotation.\n\nEach memory record requires:\n\n- stable id\n- content\n- memory class\n- assertion type\n- scope: session, branch, repo, user, organization\n- verification status\n- confidence and confidence reason\n- freshness policy\n- validity conditions\n- invalidation triggers\n- provenance events\n- evidence references\n- linked files\n- linked symbols\n- linked docs\n- linked tests\n- linked memories\n- contradiction links\n- supersession links\n- access history\n- usefulness scores\n- last verified state\n\nMemory links should be first-class records:\n\n- source memory\n- target memory or graph node\n- link type\n- strength\n- reason\n- evidence event\n- created by\n- created at\n- verification status\n\nRequired link types:\n\n- `supports`\n- `contradicts`\n- `supersedes`\n- `refines`\n- `generalizes`\n- `specializes`\n- `co_occurs_with`\n- `derived_from`\n- `applies_to`\n- `validated_by`\n- `invalidated_by`\n\n### 5. Working Memory\n\nWorking memory is per-task and short-lived. It should be explicit rather than implicit prompt accumulation.\n\nWorking memory contains:\n\n- task statement\n- interpreted intent\n- active files and symbols\n- active hypotheses\n- active failures\n- current plan\n- selected memories\n- excluded memories and reasons\n- budget decisions\n- unresolved questions\n- verification status\n\nRequired operations:\n\n- `retrieve`\n- `summarize`\n- `filter`\n- `pin`\n- `evict`\n- `expand`\n- `compress`\n- `checkpoint`\n\nWorking memory is not durable by itself. Durable value is extracted through event-backed consolidation.\n\n### 6. Consolidation Engine\n\nThe consolidation engine turns event traces into better memory.\n\nConsolidation jobs:\n\n- create episode summaries from completed tasks\n- promote repeated successful workflow traces into procedures\n- promote recurring failures into failure patterns\n- promote verified implementation facts into semantic repo memory\n- detect duplicate memories\n- detect contradictions\n- detect supersession candidates\n- demote unused or low-value memories\n- mark stale memories after graph changes\n- refresh memories whose evidence still matches current code\n- propose docs updates when memory and docs diverge\n\nConsolidation modes:\n\n- synchronous post-task consolidation for small task traces\n- background scheduled consolidation for larger histories\n- manual review mode for high-impact repo or organization memories\n- replay mode for rebuilding memory state from the event log\n\nNo consolidation job may silently rewrite high-scope memory without preserving provenance and prior state.\n\nLLM-driven consolidation:\n\nSeveral consolidation jobs (episode summary generation, procedure extraction, contradiction detection, failure-pattern extraction) cannot be implemented deterministically and require LLM inference. These jobs must follow additional constraints:\n\n- each LLM-driven job produces a proposal record, not a direct write; proposals require explicit apply or reject before altering durable memory\n- failed or malformed LLM responses must leave the prior memory state unchanged and emit a consolidation failure event\n- LLM-driven jobs run only in background or manual review modes, never on the synchronous post-task hot path\n- each job records its model, prompt hash, and response hash as part of the proposal provenance so outputs are auditable and reproducible\n- consolidation queue depth must be bounded; when the queue is full, new jobs are dropped with a log warning rather than stalling the daemon\n- cost and latency budgets for LLM consolidation should be documented per job type before Phase 6 begins; jobs exceeding budget must fall back to deterministic approximations or skip with a stale flag\n\n### 7. Retrieval Engine\n\nRetrieval must be task-aware and explainable.\n\nPipeline:\n\n1. Parse user task and classify intent.\n2. Extract literal anchors: paths, symbols, errors, commands, APIs, config keys.\n3. Resolve anchors into graph identities.\n4. Retrieve graph candidates.\n5. Retrieve memory candidates from typed streams.\n6. Retrieve relevant event episodes.\n7. Expand through bounded graph and memory neighborhoods.\n8. Score candidates.\n9. Deduplicate and compress.\n10. Return a compact bundle with inclusion reasons and expansion handles.\n\nCandidate sources:\n\n- exact path/symbol lookup\n- code graph traversal\n- doc backlinks/outgoing links\n- FTS\n- embeddings\n- event similarity\n- memory links\n- workflow similarity\n- recent active working memory\n\nRanking signals:\n\n- task-type compatibility\n- graph proximity to anchors\n- exact identifier match\n- semantic similarity\n- verification status\n- freshness\n- scope\n- evidence strength\n- contradiction/supersession state\n- past usefulness\n- recent successful reuse\n- user preference compatibility\n- token cost\n\nThe ranker should expose scores and reasons in diagnostic mode. Compact mode should include only enough explanation for an assistant to trust the result.\n\n### 8. Verification Engine\n\nThe verification engine keeps memory honest.\n\nVerification checks:\n\n- linked files still exist\n- linked symbols still exist\n- cited docs still exist\n- linked tests still exist\n- evidence text still matches when exact spans were captured\n- implementation still matches memory claim where deterministic checks are possible\n- contradicted/superseded states remain coherent\n- branch-scoped memory is not leaking into unrelated branches\n- time-bound memory has expired\n\nVerification outputs:\n\n- `verified`\n- `unverified`\n- `in_review`\n- `stale`\n- `contradicted`\n- `superseded`\n- `expired`\n- `invalidated`\n\nVerification must be incremental and tied to workspace changes. Large repos cannot tolerate full rescans for every memory.\n\n### 9. MCP Surface\n\nThe MCP contract should expose high-level assistant workflows rather than forcing clients to compose low-level graph calls.\n\nRequired workflow tools:\n\n- `get_context_capsule`\n- `prepare_change`\n- `plan_edit`\n- `trace_scenario`\n- `diagnose_failure`\n- `find_relevant_tests`\n- `impact_from_diff`\n- `get_docs_capsule`\n- `get_backlinks`\n- `expand_context`\n- `get_working_set_context`\n- `get_repo_playbook`\n- `search_symbols`\n- `search_memory`\n- `record_workflow_outcome`\n\nNew or redesigned memory tools:\n\n- `get_task_memory`\n- `save_memory`\n- `propose_memory_evolution`\n- `apply_memory_evolution`\n- `verify_memory`\n- `explain_memory`\n- `list_memory_conflicts`\n- `consolidate_session`\n- `get_memory_metrics`\n- `get_event_trace`\n\nTool surface discipline: ten new memory tools is a meaningful cognitive load for clients. Before shipping Phase 8, audit whether `propose_memory_evolution` + `apply_memory_evolution` can collapse into a single tool with an `action` parameter, and whether `verify_memory` + `explain_memory` can be unified. The goal is the smallest surface that covers all assistant workflows. Consolidate before stabilizing the MCP contract.\n\nEvery tool response should support:\n\n- compact rendering\n- full structured JSON\n- context handles\n- stable expansion targets\n- budget controls\n- diagnostic explanations where useful\n\n### 10. Human Review Surface\n\nAgents are the primary client, but durable memory needs human inspectability.\n\nRequired operator views:\n\n- memory inbox\n- proposed promotions\n- proposed contradictions\n- stale memory list\n- memory evidence view\n- event trace view\n- retrieval explanation view\n- usefulness metrics\n- workspace graph health\n- indexing health\n- consolidation queue\n\nThis can be a VS Code extension panel or local web UI. It should be a review and trust surface, not the center of the product.\n\n## Storage Design\n\nUse SQLite as the durable local store unless proven insufficient. Split logical tables cleanly.\n\nCore tables:\n\n- `workspaces`\n- `files`\n- `symbols`\n- `documents`\n- `sections`\n- `tests`\n- `graph_edges`\n- `events`\n- `event_payloads`\n- `memories`\n- `memory_links`\n- `memory_evidence`\n- `memory_accesses`\n- `memory_scores`\n- `working_memory_checkpoints`\n- `context_handles`\n- `consolidation_jobs`\n- `verification_jobs`\n\nIndexes:\n\n- graph identity indexes\n- file path indexes\n- symbol name/path indexes\n- doc section indexes\n- event task/session indexes\n- memory scope/type/status indexes\n- memory link source/target indexes\n- freshness and verification indexes\n- FTS over code summary, docs, memory, and event summaries\n- vector indexes over symbols, doc sections, memories, and event episodes\n\nThe event log should be append-only. Derived graph and memory state can be rebuilt from source files and events.\n\nEvent log compaction: append-only storage will grow unboundedly for long-running repos. The storage design must include a compaction snapshot strategy: at configurable intervals, write a full-state snapshot of graph and memory to a separate file, then truncate the event log to events after the snapshot timestamp. The snapshot format must be versioned and independently readable so the daemon can bootstrap from a snapshot without replaying the full history. Compaction must be a daemon-managed background operation, not a user-initiated manual step.\n\n## Fork Strategy\n\nThis should be a fork only if the implementation needs to break major internal assumptions. Preserve useful Lattice assets.\n\nCarry forward:\n\n- Rust daemon structure\n- MCP handling experience\n- workspace boundary protections\n- existing workflow tool vocabulary\n- graph parsing and indexing foundation\n- context handles\n- compact/full render modes\n- structured memory groundwork\n- test discipline around daemon behavior\n- VS Code extension integration pattern\n\nReplace or redesign:\n\n- memory retrieval as keyword search\n- memory as mostly isolated rows\n- workflow metrics as secondary side data\n- event capture as implicit logs instead of a first-class substrate\n- ranking paths that cannot explain why context was included\n- any storage path that cannot be replayed or verified\n\n## Implementation Plan\n\n### Phase 0: Fork Foundation\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- new repo or long-lived branch with clear successor name\n- architecture docs copied and revised\n- crate/module boundary plan\n- storage migration policy\n- compatibility policy for existing MCP tools\n- baseline benchmark suite from current Lattice workflows\n\nFork decision gate:\n\nPhase 0 ends with an explicit fork/extend decision before any structural code changes begin. Fork if any of the following are true:\n\n- the memory graph schema changes require breaking the existing `memories` table in a way that cannot be migrated without a full rebuild\n- the identity model requires changing the primary key or graph edge representation in a way that invalidates existing indexed data\n- the event log requires a new storage file or WAL mode incompatible with the current daemon's SQLite usage\n- two or more of the above apply and sharing a codebase would require sustained compatibility shims across all phases\n\nIf none of those conditions hold, extend in place on a long-lived branch and skip the repo split. Document the decision and the evidence for it before Phase 1 begins.\n\nVerification:\n\n- current Lattice daemon tests pass in the fork before major changes\n- current MCP workflow fixtures run unchanged or with documented compatibility shims\n\n### Phase 1: Unified Identity Model\n\nIdentity must precede the event log. An event that references a file, symbol, or doc by unstable name cannot be reliably replayed or used for consolidation. This phase establishes the identity substrate that all subsequent phases depend on.\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- stable ids for files, symbols, docs, sections, events, memories, and context handles\n- identity resolver for paths, symbols, headings, tests, and event refs\n- explicit identity serialization in MCP payloads\n- compatibility support for legacy name-based references\n- tests for duplicate symbol names, renamed files, moved sections, and branch changes\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- every workflow output can be expanded through a stable identity\n- ambiguous names return ambiguity diagnostics instead of arbitrary matches\n- identity resolution adds no more than 2ms P99 to hot-path tool calls\n\n### Phase 2: Event Log Substrate\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- event model\n- append-only SQLite event tables\n- event writer API\n- event reader/query API\n- event payload hashing and optional payload spillover\n- MCP/tool-call event capture using stable identities from Phase 1\n- workflow task/session correlation\n- tests for ordering, replay, corruption handling, and workspace scoping\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- every high-level MCP workflow records task, retrieval, response, and outcome events\n- event log can replay enough data to reconstruct workflow history\n- failure paths emit useful events without hiding errors\n- event write adds no more than 5ms P99 to hot-path tool calls (`prepare_change`, `get_context_capsule`); large payloads spill to a side table rather than blocking the writer\n\n### Phase 3: Memory Graph Storage\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- redesigned memory schema\n- first-class memory links\n- first-class memory evidence\n- memory access/use history\n- contradiction and supersession as graph edges\n- memory stream/type taxonomy\n- migration/import path from existing Lattice memory rows\n- tests for memory creation, linking, contradiction, supersession, stale state, and replay\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- memory can represent atomic notes, claims, workflow outcomes, procedures, preferences, and counter-memory\n- every memory can explain its evidence and relationship state\n\n### Phase 4: Retrieval V1\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- task intent classifier\n- anchor extractor and resolver\n- hybrid candidate retrieval from graph, docs, memories, and events\n- scoring model with graph proximity, semantic similarity, freshness, scope, verification, and usefulness\n- compact response shaper\n- diagnostic response mode with ranking reasons\n- benchmark suite for realistic assistant tasks\n\nScope constraint: Phase 4 retrieval must not depend on the formal working memory state model defined in Phase 5. Retrieval returns bundles into the calling context; working memory formalization is layered on top in Phase 5. Design the retrieval output schema to be forward-compatible with working memory checkpoints without requiring a Phase 4 rewrite.\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- memory retrieval no longer depends on keyword search as the primary path\n- workflow tools return memory with inclusion reasons and stable expansion handles\n- irrelevant memory rate is measured and regressed\n\n### Phase 5: Working Memory\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- explicit per-task working memory state\n- operations for retrieve, summarize, filter, pin, evict, expand, compress, and checkpoint\n- MCP surface for inspecting current working memory\n- automatic event capture for included and excluded context\n- tests for token budgets, pinned context, eviction, and checkpoint restore\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- workflow tools can show what context is active, why it is active, and what was intentionally excluded\n- context management is no longer just prompt accumulation\n\n### Phase 6: Consolidation Engine\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- session consolidation jobs\n- episode summary generation\n- memory promotion rules\n- duplicate detection\n- contradiction detection\n- supersession proposal\n- procedure extraction from repeated successful workflows\n- failure-pattern extraction from repeated diagnostics\n- manual review queue for high-scope memory changes\n- replay-safe job execution\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- completed tasks produce useful episode memories\n- repeated successful traces produce procedural memories\n- consolidation is auditable, reversible, and test-covered\n\n### Phase 7: Verification And Freshness\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- incremental memory verification against graph changes\n- file/symbol/doc/test existence checks\n- exact-span evidence validation where available\n- branch and workspace scope enforcement\n- time-bound expiry\n- stale memory surfacing in workflow bundles\n- verification jobs and operator diagnostics\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- stale or contradicted memory cannot appear as normal trusted guidance\n- verification results are explainable and queryable\n\n### Phase 8: Workflow Engine V2\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- redesigned versions of context, change planning, scenario tracing, failure diagnosis, docs retrieval, test selection, impact analysis, and playbook generation\n- workflow composition over graph, event, memory, and working-memory substrates\n- one-call edit-planning bundles with code, docs, tests, memory, risks, and verification commands\n- workflow outcome recording integrated by default\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- common coding tasks require fewer manual discovery calls\n- workflows produce enough context to implement and verify changes without broad file dumping\n\n### Phase 9: Metrics And Evaluation\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- retrieval relevance metrics\n- memory usefulness metrics\n- context token efficiency metrics\n- stale/contradicted memory surfacing metrics\n- test-pass-after-plan metrics\n- repeated-failure reduction metrics\n- benchmark tasks with golden expected anchors\n- regression dashboard or CLI report\n\nRequired metrics:\n\n- tool calls per successful task\n- irrelevant files opened per task\n- relevant anchor recall\n- memory inclusion precision\n- memory later-used rate\n- stale memory surfaced rate\n- contradiction missed rate\n- tests recommended versus tests needed\n- workflow success after first plan\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- changes to retrieval, memory, and workflow behavior can be evaluated without relying on anecdote\n\n### Phase 10: Human Review And Extension UX\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- VS Code or local web review UI\n- memory inbox\n- promotion queue\n- contradiction queue\n- stale memory view\n- event trace view\n- retrieval explanation view\n- consolidation job health\n- indexing health\n- one-click accept/reject for memory evolution proposals\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- operators can understand and correct durable memory without reading raw SQLite rows\n\n### Phase 11: Hardening\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- large-repo performance tests\n- concurrency tests\n- recovery tests\n- corrupted-event handling\n- partial-index handling\n- workspace-boundary tests\n- migration tests\n- MCP schema compatibility tests\n- documentation for setup, operations, memory semantics, and workflow behavior\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- the system is production-grade for repeated agent use, not only successful on a toy repo\n\n## MCP Tool Contract Principles\n\nEvery assistant-facing tool should return:\n\n- `overview`\n- ranked pivots\n- relevant context\n- memory highlights\n- event episodes where relevant\n- suggested next expansion\n- stable handles\n- risks or uncertainty\n- compact/full render choice\n- structured payload\n\nEvery memory-bearing response should identify:\n\n- why the memory was included\n- evidence strength\n- verification status\n- freshness status\n- contradiction/supersession state\n- scope\n- expansion target\n\nEvery workflow tool should record:\n\n- inputs\n- resolved anchors\n- selected candidates\n- excluded high-scoring candidates where useful\n- response summary\n- downstream use events\n\n## Documentation Requirements\n\nRequired docs:\n\n- successor architecture overview\n- MCP contract reference\n- memory model reference\n- event log design\n- consolidation design\n- retrieval/ranking design\n- verification/freshness design\n- operator guide\n- migration guide from Lattice\n- benchmark/evaluation guide\n- extension review UI guide\n\nDocs must be updated in the same change as public behavior changes.\n\n## Testing Requirements\n\nRequired test families:\n\n- storage migration tests\n- event append/replay tests\n- graph identity tests\n- parser tests\n- memory link tests\n- contradiction/supersession tests\n- stale verification tests\n- retrieval ranking golden tests\n- workflow golden tests\n- MCP schema tests\n- compact/full render tests\n- concurrency tests\n- large fixture performance tests\n- extension compile/UI smoke tests\n\nNo phase is complete until its failure paths are tested.\n\n## Measurable Success Criteria\n\nThe fork should beat current Lattice on:\n\n- lower tool calls per coding task\n- lower irrelevant context inclusion\n- higher relevant file/symbol recall\n- higher memory reuse precision\n- lower stale memory surfacing\n- better repeated-failure avoidance\n- better test recommendation accuracy\n- better user correction retention\n- faster diagnosis from failures\n- more explainable context selection\n\nInitial targets:\n\n- 30 percent fewer discovery tool calls on benchmark tasks\n- 40 percent fewer irrelevant file reads\n- 80 percent memory inclusion precision on curated memory benchmarks\n- zero trusted display of known contradicted memory\n- zero trusted display of known stale memory without stale label\n- 90 percent correct relevant-test recommendation on curated tasks\n\n## Risks\n\n### Opaque Memory Mutation\n\nRisk: automatic consolidation corrupts durable repo knowledge.\n\nControl: event-backed proposals, reversible updates, provenance, review queue for high-scope changes, and replay.\n\n### Ranking Complexity\n\nRisk: ranking becomes hard to reason about.\n\nControl: keep ranker feature-based and inspectable before any learned policy. Expose diagnostic scores.\n\n### Event Log Growth\n\nRisk: event storage becomes too large or slow.\n\nControl: append compact summaries on hot paths, spill large payloads, index by task/session/workspace, and support compaction snapshots.\n\n### Stale Memory Leakage\n\nRisk: agents trust outdated memory.\n\nControl: freshness indexes, graph-change-triggered verification, stale labels in all memory surfaces, and tests that stale memory cannot rank as trusted.\n\n### Scope Leakage\n\nRisk: user/org/branch memories leak into wrong workspaces.\n\nControl: scope-aware queries, enforced filters in store APIs, and negative tests.\n\n### Overfitting To Current Repo\n\nRisk: benchmarks pass only for Lattice-like Rust/TypeScript repos.\n\nControl: fixture repos across languages, repo sizes, and documentation styles.\n\n## First Implementation Slice\n\nThe first real slice should be event log plus memory graph, not UI.\n\nImplement in this order:\n\n1. Event model and append-only store.\n2. MCP workflow event capture.\n3. Memory graph schema and migration/import from existing rows.\n4. Memory access/use history.\n5. Retrieval V1 with graph-aware memory ranking.\n6. Compact memory inclusion reasons in existing workflow tools.\n7. Session consolidation into episode memories.\n8. Verification and stale surfacing tests.\n\nThis slice creates the substrate that all later intelligence depends on. Building UI or learned policy before this substrate would create debt instead of capability.\n\n## Final Target\n\nThe final system should make a coding agent feel like it is operating in a workspace that remembers accurately:\n\n- what the repo contains\n- why the repo is shaped that way\n- what was tried before\n- what failed before\n- what tests prove behavior\n- what memories are trustworthy\n- what memories are stale\n- what context matters now\n- what should be done next\n\nThat is the measurable leap over a code graph plus ad hoc memory store.\n","numLines":969,"startLine":1,"totalLines":969}}}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/R05.log:16:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MJFTENAUNhRVWjLDoqPYuB","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Cognitive Workspace Architecture\n2\t\n3\t## Summary\n4\t\n5\tThis document is the Phase 0 successor architecture overview required by [docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-0-fork-foundation](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-0-fork-foundation). It translates the design contract from [## Design Thesis](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#design-thesis), [## System Architecture](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#system-architecture), and [## Storage Design](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#storage-design) into an in-place branch architecture for `feat/cognitive-workspace`, consistent with [docs/architecture/2026-05-16-fork-or-extend-decision.md#Decision](./2026-05-16-fork-or-extend-decision.md#decision).\n6\t\n7\tThe binding model is a shared cognition substrate with three first-class sources of truth:\n8\t\n9\t1. workspace graph\n10\t2. event log\n11\t3. memory graph\n12\t\n13\tThese substrates must share identity, storage, and ranking primitives. Phase sequencing can add new crates and tables over time, but it must not let each substrate drift into its own incompatible naming, storage, or retrieval contract.\n14\t\n15\tThe MCP compatibility implications of this architecture are defined in [docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-compatibility-policy.md](./2026-05-16-mcp-compatibility-policy.md).\n16\t\n17\t## Workspace graph\n18\t\n19\tPer [## System Architecture](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#system-architecture), the workspace graph remains the source of truth for code, docs, tests, and operational surfaces. The current implementation already owns most of the graph substrate in `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/graph`, `src/storage`, `src/indexer`, and `src/query`; later phases may split some ownership into planned crates such as `lattice-identity`, but the graph contract remains continuous.\n20\t\n21\t### Node families\n22\t\n23\t| Node family | Current or planned owner | Notes |\n24\t|---|---|---|\n25\t| `File` | Current: `lattice-core::graph`, `lattice-core::storage`, `lattice-core::watcher` | Existing file indexing and storage stay authoritative. |\n26\t| `Directory` | Current: `lattice-core::workspace`; planned stronger identity in `lattice-identity` | Currently implicit in workspace and path handling; Phase 1 should make directory identity explicit where useful. |\n27\t| `Symbol` | Current: `lattice-core::symbols`, `lattice-core::graph`, `lattice-core::storage` | Existing `SymbolId { file, name, byte_offset }` stays the durable basis. |\n28\t| `Type` | Current: `lattice-core::graph` via symbol parsing; planned richer type identity in `lattice-identity` | Remains graph-backed rather than a separate storage system. |\n29\t| `Module` | Current: `lattice-core::graph` and parser/indexer paths | Module boundaries continue to derive from language-specific graph ingestion. |\n30\t| `Test` | Current: workflow/intelligence and graph query surfaces; planned stronger first-class storage in graph tables | Existing test discovery remains, but `Test` identity must become explicit for verification and retrieval. |\n31\t| `Document` | Current: query/intelligence/doc capsules; planned stronger graph persistence | Markdown and durable docs stay part of the same graph substrate, not a parallel doc-only index. |\n32\t| `Section` | Current: docs retrieval flows; planned stronger identity in `lattice-identity` and graph storage | Stable section identity is required for follow-up expansion and stale-doc detection. |\n33\t| `ConfigKey` | Current: parser/query extraction where available; planned broader ingestion coverage | Remains a graph node because retrieval must connect config facts to code, docs, and workflows. |\n34\t| `Command` | Planned ingestion in graph and event-linked storage | Commands need graph identity because workflows, procedures, and diagnostics cite them repeatedly. |\n35\t| `Route` | Planned graph/indexer extension | Route nodes belong in the workspace graph because docs, tests, and runtime surfaces link to them. |\n36\t| `Schema` | Planned graph/indexer extension | Schema nodes cover API, config, and storage contracts needed by impact analysis and docs checks. |\n37\t| `Package` | Current workspace/build metadata ingestion; planned stronger graph persistence | Package ownership stays tied to workspace/build parsing rather than memory. |\n38\t| `BuildTarget` | Planned graph/indexer extension | Build targets are graph facts used by tests, diagnostics, and procedures. |\n39\t| `RuntimeSurface` | Planned graph plus event references | Runtime surfaces connect code, routes, commands, diagnostics, and operations. |\n40\t\n41\t### Edge families\n42\t\n43\t| Edge family | Current or planned owner | Notes |\n44\t|---|---|---|\n45\t| `contains` | Current graph/store | Core file, symbol, and doc containment stays graph-native. |\n46\t| `imports` | Current graph/store | Already part of code graph semantics. |\n47\t| `calls` | Current graph/store | Already part of code graph semantics. |\n48\t| `implements` | Current graph/store | Existing type and symbol relations extend here. |\n49\t| `extends` | Current graph/store | Existing type and symbol relations extend here. |\n50\t| `type_ref` | Current graph/store | Type reference edges stay in graph, not memory. |\n51\t| `tested_by` | Current workflows; planned first-class graph/storage edge | Needed for deterministic test recommendation and verification. |\n52\t| `documents` | Current docs/workflow logic; planned first-class graph/storage edge | Connects doc sections to code and schema nodes. |\n53\t| `mentions` | Current docs/workflow logic; planned first-class graph/storage edge | Enables backlinks, stale-doc detection, and retrieval evidence. |\n54\t| `depends_on` | Current graph/store | Remains the default impact-analysis edge family. |\n55\t| `configured_by` | Planned graph/storage edge | Connects config keys, schemas, and runtime surfaces. |\n56\t| `generated_by` | Planned graph/storage edge | Tracks generated assets and machine-produced files. |\n57\t| `co_changed_with` | Planned event-derived graph edge | Materialized from event history and diffs, but stored as graph state for bounded traversal. |\n58\t| `stale_against` | Planned verification-derived graph edge | Marks docs or memories whose claims have drifted against workspace facts. |\n59\t\n60\tThe graph continues to answer exact identity lookup, bounded neighborhood traversal, impact analysis, backlinks, and freshness queries. What changes is breadth and identity discipline, not the fundamental responsibility boundary.\n61\t\n62\t## Event log\n63\t\n64\tPer [## System Architecture](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#system-architecture), [## Storage Design](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#storage-design), and [### Phase 2: Event Log Substrate](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-2-event-log-substrate), the event log becomes the append-only record of assistant and workflow activity.\n65\t\n66\t### Required event types\n67\t\n68\tThe event taxonomy is fixed at Phase 0 so later phases do not drift:\n69\t\n70\t- `AssistantTaskStarted`\n71\t- `ToolCalled`\n72\t- `ToolResult`\n73\t- `ContextBundleReturned`\n74\t- `MemoryRetrieved`\n75\t- `MemoryExpanded`\n76\t- `PlanCreated`\n77\t- `FileRead`\n78\t- `PatchApplied`\n79\t- `TestRunStarted`\n80\t- `TestRunCompleted`\n81\t- `DiagnosticObserved`\n82\t- `UserCorrection`\n83\t- `UserPreferenceObserved`\n84\t- `WorkflowSucceeded`\n85\t- `WorkflowFailed`\n86\t- `MemoryCreated`\n87\t- `MemoryUpdated`\n88\t- `MemoryInvalidated`\n89\t- `MemoryConsolidated`\n90\t\n91\t### Invariants\n92\t\n93\t- The event log is append-only. Updates happen by appending new events, never by mutating historical rows in place.\n94\t- Events carry stable workspace, branch, session, task, actor, and referenced-identity metadata so replay remains deterministic across graph and memory evolution.\n95\t- Large payloads are hash-addressed and may spill to side storage, but every event row still carries a compact summary and payload hash.\n96\t- Event history is for audit, replay, metrics, consolidation, and retrieval. It is not a prompt dump surface.\n97\t\n98\t### Compaction and snapshots\n99\t\n100\tThe append-only invariant does not permit unbounded hot-path replay. Per [## Storage Design](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#storage-design), compaction is handled through versioned snapshots:\n101\t\n102\t- the daemon writes a full graph-and-memory snapshot at configured compaction points\n103\t- the snapshot is independently readable and versioned\n104\t- the live event log is truncated only to post-snapshot history\n105\t- bootstrap may start from the newest valid snapshot plus post-snapshot replay\n106\t\n107\tCompaction is therefore a state-transfer optimization, not a rewrite of history semantics.\n108\t\n109\t### Performance budget\n110\t\n111\tPhase 2 establishes a hard hot-path budget: event writing adds no more than 5 ms P99 to `prepare_change` and `get_context_capsule`, per [### Phase 2: Event Log Substrate](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-2-event-log-substrate). That budget binds later event-schema, storage, and payload-shaping decisions. If a new event field or serialization path threatens the budget, it must move to spillover payload storage, asynchronous enrichment, or derived tables rather than slowing the write path.\n112\t\n113\t### Ownership\n114\t\n115\tCurrent and planned ownership is intentionally split:\n116\t\n117\t- current daemon integration: `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc` records tool and workflow boundaries\n118\t- planned storage and APIs: `lattice-events` or equivalent Phase 2 module owns append-only tables, readers, writers, and replay helpers\n119\t- shared identity: `lattice-identity` or equivalent Phase 1 module resolves referenced files, symbols, docs, sections, memories, and handles\n120\t\n121\t## Memory graph\n122\t\n123\tPer [## Design Thesis](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#design-thesis) and [### 4. Memory Graph](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#4-memory-graph), durable memory is a typed claim graph over workspace and event history, not an isolated table of note rows.\n124\t\n125\t### Memory classes\n126\t\n127\tThe planned first-class memory classes are:\n128\t\n129\t- `Observation`\n130\t- `Decision`\n131\t- `Constraint`\n132\t- `Pattern`\n133\t- `AntiPattern`\n134\t- `WorkflowOutcome`\n135\t- `FailurePattern`\n136\t- `Procedure`\n137\t- `Preference`\n138\t- `ArchitectureInvariant`\n139\t- `DocsContract`\n140\t- `OpenQuestion`\n141\t- `CounterMemory`\n142\t\n143\tCurrent structured memory already covers several trust and relationship fields in `memories`; later phases normalize that into richer graph storage without breaking existing rows, as required by [docs/architecture/2026-05-16-fork-or-extend-decision.md#Migration-implication](./2026-05-16-fork-or-extend-decision.md#migration-implication).\n144\t\n145\t### Link types\n146\t\n147\tMemory links are first-class records, not JSON-only annotations. The planned link vocabulary from [### 4. Memory Graph](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#4-memory-graph) is:\n148\t\n149\t- `supports`\n150\t- `contradicts`\n151\t- `supersedes`\n152\t- `refines`\n153\t- `generalizes`\n154\t- `specializes`\n155\t- `co_occurs_with`\n156\t- `derived_from`\n157\t- `applies_to`\n158\t- `validated_by`\n159\t- `invalidated_by`\n160\t\n161\tEach link carries source, target, strength, reason, evidence event, creator, creation time, and verification status.\n162\t\n163\t### Evidence model\n164\t\n165\tEvery durable memory requires evidence. The minimum evidence model is shared across direct memory writes, workflow outcome recording, consolidation proposals, and verification:\n166\t\n167\t- provenance events identify where the claim came from\n168\t- evidence references point at files, symbols, docs, tests, events, or exact spans\n169\t- verification status describes current trust state\n170\t- freshness policy and validity conditions explain when the claim should be rechecked\n171\t- contradiction and supersession state remain explicit instead of hidden in ranking\n172\t- usefulness and access history stay queryable for retrieval and evaluation\n173\t\n174\tCurrent groundwork for this model already exists in [docs/architecture/2026-04-11-structured-memory.md#Data-Model](./2026-04-11-structured-memory.md#data-model), [#Persistence-And-Migration](./2026-04-11-structured-memory.md#persistence-and-migration), and [#Explainability-Boundary](./2026-04-11-structured-memory.md#explainability-boundary).\n175\t\n176\t### `CounterMemory` distinction\n177\t\n178\t`CounterMemory` is a first-class memory record asserting that another memory is wrong, stale in meaning, or no longer applicable. That is a stronger construct than a `contradicts` link:\n179\t\n180\t- a `contradicts` link expresses a relationship between two existing memories\n181\t- a `CounterMemory` record is its own claim with evidence, scope, verification, and lifecycle\n182\t- a `CounterMemory` can itself be contradicted, superseded, refreshed, or invalidated\n183\t\n184\tUse a contradiction edge when the relationship is enough. Use `CounterMemory` when the counter-claim must be retrieved, reviewed, and verified as a standalone fact.\n185\t\n186\t### Ownership\n187\t\n188\t- current compatibility layer: `lattice-core::memory`\n189\t- current assistant-facing ranking and shaping: `lattice-core::intelligence`\n190\t- planned normalized storage and APIs: `lattice-memory`\n191\t- planned verification and consolidation integration: Phase 6 and Phase 7 modules over shared memory and event identities\n192\t\n193\t## Shared substrate primitives\n194\t\n195\tThe three substrates are separate sources of truth, but they are not allowed to invent separate foundational primitives.\n196\t\n197\t### Identity\n198\t\n199\tIdentity is shared first. Files, symbols, docs, sections, events, memories, tests, and context handles need stable ids that can cross graph rows, event rows, memory evidence, and MCP payloads. Phase 1 formalizes that on top of the current `SymbolId` basis described in [docs/architecture/2026-04-11-stable-follow-up-handles.md#Durable-Identity-Basis](./2026-04-11-stable-follow-up-handles.md#durable-identity-basis).\n200\t\n201\t### Storage\n202\t\n203\tStorage is shared second. Per [## Storage Design](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#storage-design), SQLite remains the durable local store. The substrates may use separate logical tables and, where justified, separate database files such as `.lattice/events.db`, but they must share migration discipline, replayability, workspace scoping, and snapshot compatibility. Derived indexes, FTS, and vector indexes remain replaceable accelerators rather than hidden sources of truth.\n204\t\n205\t### Ranking and retrieval\n206\t\n207\tRanking is shared third. Retrieval across graph, events, and memories must use one explainable scoring model family with task compatibility, identity anchors, proximity, verification, freshness, contradiction state, usefulness, and token cost as first-class signals. Memory ranking cannot ignore graph proximity, and graph ranking cannot ignore validated memory or event evidence. This is the core operational consequence of [## Design Thesis](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#design-thesis).\n208\t\n209\t### Shared MCP contract consequences\n210\t\n211\tBecause the substrates share primitives, assistant-facing workflows keep one bounded contract:\n212\t\n213\t- compact and full render modes stay consistent\n214\t- context handles and stable expansion targets remain portable across graph, event, and memory-backed tools\n215\t- diagnostic explanations use the same trust and inclusion vocabulary\n216\t- backward compatibility is enforced at the tool boundary, not left to each substrate team\n217\t\n218\tThe detailed compatibility rules live in [docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-compatibility-policy.md#Backward-compatibility](./2026-05-16-mcp-compatibility-policy.md#backward-compatibility).\n219\t\n220\t## Reference to existing docs\n221\t\n222\tThis overview is the successor umbrella document. It does not replace the verified lower-level notes below; it binds them together and points later phases at the right detailed contract.\n223\t\n224\t- [docs/architecture/2026-04-11-structured-memory.md#Summary](./2026-04-11-structured-memory.md#summary)\n225\t- [docs/architecture/2026-04-11-structured-memory.md#Data-Model](./2026-04-11-structured-memory.md#data-model)\n226\t- [docs/architecture/2026-04-11-structured-memory.md#Persistence-And-Migration](./2026-04-11-structured-memory.md#persistence-and-migration)\n227\t- [docs/architecture/2026-04-11-stable-follow-up-handles.md#Contract](./2026-04-11-stable-follow-up-handles.md#contract)\n228\t- [docs/architecture/2026-04-11-storage-and-search-backends.md#Contract-Notes](./2026-04-11-storage-and-search-backends.md#contract-notes)\n229\t- [docs/architecture/2026-04-11-patch-oriented-planning.md#Bundle-Shape](./2026-04-11-patch-oriented-planning.md#bundle-shape)\n230\t- [docs/architecture/2026-04-11-scenario-tracing.md#Contract](./2026-04-11-scenario-tracing.md#contract)\n231\t\n232\tThose notes remain authoritative for already-implemented behavior. This document is authoritative for the successor architecture boundary across the three substrates.\n233\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4cb46183-2e7c-4dbc-b888-4ae03f7bba08","uuid":"5c8af64f-783a-4e47-b3be-459a529078d1","timestamp":"2026-05-17T03:27:48.386Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/architecture/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-architecture.md","content":"# Cognitive Workspace Architecture\n\n## Summary\n\nThis document is the Phase 0 successor architecture overview required by [docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-0-fork-foundation](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-0-fork-foundation). It translates the design contract from [## Design Thesis](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#design-thesis), [## System Architecture](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#system-architecture), and [## Storage Design](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#storage-design) into an in-place branch architecture for `feat/cognitive-workspace`, consistent with [docs/architecture/2026-05-16-fork-or-extend-decision.md#Decision](./2026-05-16-fork-or-extend-decision.md#decision).\n\nThe binding model is a shared cognition substrate with three first-class sources of truth:\n\n1. workspace graph\n2. event log\n3. memory graph\n\nThese substrates must share identity, storage, and ranking primitives. Phase sequencing can add new crates and tables over time, but it must not let each substrate drift into its own incompatible naming, storage, or retrieval contract.\n\nThe MCP compatibility implications of this architecture are defined in [docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-compatibility-policy.md](./2026-05-16-mcp-compatibility-policy.md).\n\n## Workspace graph\n\nPer [## System Architecture](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#system-architecture), the workspace graph remains the source of truth for code, docs, tests, and operational surfaces. The current implementation already owns most of the graph substrate in `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/graph`, `src/storage`, `src/indexer`, and `src/query`; later phases may split some ownership into planned crates such as `lattice-identity`, but the graph contract remains continuous.\n\n### Node families\n\n| Node family | Current or planned owner | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `File` | Current: `lattice-core::graph`, `lattice-core::storage`, `lattice-core::watcher` | Existing file indexing and storage stay authoritative. |\n| `Directory` | Current: `lattice-core::workspace`; planned stronger identity in `lattice-identity` | Currently implicit in workspace and path handling; Phase 1 should make directory identity explicit where useful. |\n| `Symbol` | Current: `lattice-core::symbols`, `lattice-core::graph`, `lattice-core::storage` | Existing `SymbolId { file, name, byte_offset }` stays the durable basis. |\n| `Type` | Current: `lattice-core::graph` via symbol parsing; planned richer type identity in `lattice-identity` | Remains graph-backed rather than a separate storage system. |\n| `Module` | Current: `lattice-core::graph` and parser/indexer paths | Module boundaries continue to derive from language-specific graph ingestion. |\n| `Test` | Current: workflow/intelligence and graph query surfaces; planned stronger first-class storage in graph tables | Existing test discovery remains, but `Test` identity must become explicit for verification and retrieval. |\n| `Document` | Current: query/intelligence/doc capsules; planned stronger graph persistence | Markdown and durable docs stay part of the same graph substrate, not a parallel doc-only index. |\n| `Section` | Current: docs retrieval flows; planned stronger identity in `lattice-identity` and graph storage | Stable section identity is required for follow-up expansion and stale-doc detection. |\n| `ConfigKey` | Current: parser/query extraction where available; planned broader ingestion coverage | Remains a graph node because retrieval must connect config facts to code, docs, and workflows. |\n| `Command` | Planned ingestion in graph and event-linked storage | Commands need graph identity because workflows, procedures, and diagnostics cite them repeatedly. |\n| `Route` | Planned graph/indexer extension | Route nodes belong in the workspace graph because docs, tests, and runtime surfaces link to them. |\n| `Schema` | Planned graph/indexer extension | Schema nodes cover API, config, and storage contracts needed by impact analysis and docs checks. |\n| `Package` | Current workspace/build metadata ingestion; planned stronger graph persistence | Package ownership stays tied to workspace/build parsing rather than memory. |\n| `BuildTarget` | Planned graph/indexer extension | Build targets are graph facts used by tests, diagnostics, and procedures. |\n| `RuntimeSurface` | Planned graph plus event references | Runtime surfaces connect code, routes, commands, diagnostics, and operations. |\n\n### Edge families\n\n| Edge family | Current or planned owner | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `contains` | Current graph/store | Core file, symbol, and doc containment stays graph-native. |\n| `imports` | Current graph/store | Already part of code graph semantics. |\n| `calls` | Current graph/store | Already part of code graph semantics. |\n| `implements` | Current graph/store | Existing type and symbol relations extend here. |\n| `extends` | Current graph/store | Existing type and symbol relations extend here. |\n| `type_ref` | Current graph/store | Type reference edges stay in graph, not memory. |\n| `tested_by` | Current workflows; planned first-class graph/storage edge | Needed for deterministic test recommendation and verification. |\n| `documents` | Current docs/workflow logic; planned first-class graph/storage edge | Connects doc sections to code and schema nodes. |\n| `mentions` | Current docs/workflow logic; planned first-class graph/storage edge | Enables backlinks, stale-doc detection, and retrieval evidence. |\n| `depends_on` | Current graph/store | Remains the default impact-analysis edge family. |\n| `configured_by` | Planned graph/storage edge | Connects config keys, schemas, and runtime surfaces. |\n| `generated_by` | Planned graph/storage edge | Tracks generated assets and machine-produced files. |\n| `co_changed_with` | Planned event-derived graph edge | Materialized from event history and diffs, but stored as graph state for bounded traversal. |\n| `stale_against` | Planned verification-derived graph edge | Marks docs or memories whose claims have drifted against workspace facts. |\n\nThe graph continues to answer exact identity lookup, bounded neighborhood traversal, impact analysis, backlinks, and freshness queries. What changes is breadth and identity discipline, not the fundamental responsibility boundary.\n\n## Event log\n\nPer [## System Architecture](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#system-architecture), [## Storage Design](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#storage-design), and [### Phase 2: Event Log Substrate](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-2-event-log-substrate), the event log becomes the append-only record of assistant and workflow aic notes, claims, workflow outcomes, procedures, preferences, and counter-memory\n647\t- every memory can explain its evidence and relationship state\n648\t\n649\t### Phase 4: Retrieval V1\n650\t\n651\tDeliverables:\n652\t\n653\t- task intent classifier\n654\t- anchor extractor and resolver\n655\t- hybrid candidate retrieval from graph, docs, memories, and events\n656\t- scoring model with graph proximity, semantic similarity, freshness, scope, verification, and usefulness\n657\t- compact response shaper\n658\t- diagnostic response mode with ranking reasons\n659\t- benchmark suite for realistic assistant tasks\n660\t\n661\tScope constraint: Phase 4 retrieval must not depend on the formal working memory state model defined in Phase 5. Retrieval returns bundles into the calling context; working memory formalization is layered on top in Phase 5. Design the retrieval output schema to be forward-compatible with working memory checkpoints without requiring a Phase 4 rewrite.\n662\t\n663\tDefinition of done:\n664\t\n665\t- memory retrieval no longer depends on keyword search as the primary path\n666\t- workflow tools return memory with inclusion reasons and stable expansion handles\n667\t- irrelevant memory rate is measured and regressed\n668\t\n669\t### Phase 5: Working Memory\n670\t\n671\tDeliverables:\n672\t\n673\t- explicit per-task working memory state\n674\t- operations for retrieve, summarize, filter, pin, evict, expand, compress, and checkpoint\n675\t- MCP surface for inspecting current working memory\n676\t- automatic event capture for included and excluded context\n677\t- tests for token budgets, pinned context, eviction, and checkpoint restore\n678\t\n679\tDefinition of done:\n680\t\n681\t- workflow tools can show what context is active, why it is active, and what was intentionally excluded\n682\t- context management is no longer just prompt accumulation\n683\t\n684\t### Phase 6: Consolidation Engine\n685\t\n686\tDeliverables:\n687\t\n688\t- session consolidation jobs\n689\t- episode summary generation\n690\t- memory promotion rules\n691\t- duplicate detection\n692\t- contradiction detection\n693\t- supersession proposal\n694\t- procedure extraction from repeated successful workflows\n695\t- failure-pattern extraction from repeated diagnostics\n696\t- manual review queue for high-scope memory changes\n697\t- replay-safe job execution\n698\t\n699\tDefinition of done:\n700\t\n701\t- completed tasks produce useful episode memories\n702\t- repeated successful traces produce procedural memories\n703\t- consolidation is auditable, reversible, and test-covered\n704\t\n705\t### Phase 7: Verification And Freshness\n706\t\n707\tDeliverables:\n708\t\n709\t- incremental memory verification against graph changes\n710\t- file/symbol/doc/test existence checks\n711\t- exact-span evidence validation where available\n712\t- branch and workspace scope enforcement\n713\t- time-bound expiry\n714\t- stale memory surfacing in workflow bundles\n715\t- verification jobs and operator diagnostics\n716\t\n717\tDefinition of done:\n718\t\n719\t- stale or contradicted memory cannot appear as normal trusted guidance\n720\t- verification results are explainable and queryable\n721\t\n722\t### Phase 8: Workflow Engine V2\n723\t\n724\tDeliverables:\n725\t\n726\t- redesigned versions of context, change planning, scenario tracing, failure diagnosis, docs retrieval, test selection, impact analysis, and playbook generation\n727\t- workflow composition over graph, event, memory, and working-memory substrates\n728\t- one-call edit-planning bundles with code, docs, tests, memory, risks, and verification commands\n729\t- workflow outcome recording integrated by default\n730\t\n731\tDefinition of done:\n732\t\n733\t- common coding tasks require fewer manual discovery calls\n734\t- workflows produce enough context to implement and verify changes without broad file dumping\n735\t\n736\t### Phase 9: Metrics And Evaluation\n737\t\n738\tDeliverables:\n739\t\n740\t- retrieval relevance metrics\n741\t- memory usefulness metrics\n742\t- context token efficiency metrics\n743\t- stale/contradicted memory surfacing metrics\n744\t- test-pass-after-plan metrics\n745\t- repeated-failure reduction metrics\n746\t- benchmark tasks with golden expected anchors\n747\t- regression dashboard or CLI report\n748\t\n749\tRequired metrics:\n750\t\n751\t- tool calls per successful task\n752\t- irrelevant files opened per task\n753\t- relevant anchor recall\n754\t- memory inclusion precision\n755\t- memory later-used rate\n756\t- stale memory surfaced rate\n757\t- contradiction missed rate\n758\t- tests recommended versus tests needed\n759\t- workflow success after first plan\n760\t\n761\tDefinition of done:\n762\t\n763\t- changes to retrieval, memory, and workflow behavior can be evaluated without relying on anecdote\n764\t\n765\t### Phase 10: Human Review And Extension UX\n766\t\n767\tDeliverables:\n768\t\n769\t- VS Code or local web review UI\n770\t- memory inbox\n771\t- promotion queue\n772\t- contradiction queue\n773\t- stale memory view\n774\t- event trace view\n775\t- retrieval explanation view\n776\t- consolidation job health\n777\t- indexing health\n778\t- one-click accept/reject for memory evolution proposals\n779\t\n780\tDefinition of done:\n781\t\n782\t- operators can understand and correct durable memory without reading raw SQLite rows\n783\t\n784\t### Phase 11: Hardening\n785\t\n786\tDeliverables:\n787\t\n788\t- large-repo performance tests\n789\t- concurrency tests\n790\t- recovery tests\n791\t- corrupted-event handling\n792\t- partial-index handling\n793\t- workspace-boundary tests\n794\t- migration tests\n795\t- MCP schema compatibility tests\n796\t- documentation for setup, operations, memory semantics, and workflow behavior\n797\t\n798\tDefinition of done:\n799\t\n800\t- the system is production-grade for repeated agent use, not only successful on a toy repo\n801\t\n802\t## MCP Tool Contract Principles\n803\t\n804\tEvery assistant-facing tool should return:\n805\t\n806\t- `overview`\n807\t- ranked pivots\n808\t- relevant context\n809\t- memory highlights\n810\t- event episodes where relevant\n811\t- suggested next expansion\n812\t- stable handles\n813\t- risks or uncertainty\n814\t- compact/full render choice\n815\t- structured payload\n816\t\n817\tEvery memory-bearing response should identify:\n818\t\n819\t- why the memory was included\n820\t- evidence strength\n821\t- verification status\n822\t- freshness status\n823\t- contradiction/supersession state\n824\t- scope\n825\t- expansion target\n826\t\n827\tEvery workflow tool should record:\n828\t\n829\t- inputs\n830\t- resolved anchors\n831\t- selected candidates\n832\t- excluded high-scoring candidates where useful\n833\t- response summary\n834\t- downstream use events\n835\t\n836\t## Documentation Requirements\n837\t\n838\tRequired docs:\n839\t\n840\t- successor architecture overview\n841\t- MCP contract reference\n842\t- memory model reference\n843\t- event log design\n844\t- consolidation design\n845\t- retrieval/ranking design\n846\t- verification/freshness design\n847\t- operator guide\n848\t- migration guide from Lattice\n849\t- benchmark/evaluation guide\n850\t- extension review UI guide\n851\t\n852\tDocs must be updated in the same change as public behavior changes.\n853\t\n854\t## Testing Requirements\n855\t\n856\tRequired test families:\n857\t\n858\t- storage migration tests\n859\t- event append/replay tests\n860\t- graph identity tests\n861\t- parser tests\n862\t- memory link tests\n863\t- contradiction/supersession tests\n864\t- stale verification tests\n865\t- retrieval ranking golden tests\n866\t- workflow golden tests\n867\t- MCP schema tests\n868\t- compact/full render tests\n869\t- concurrency tests\n870\t- large fixture performance tests\n871\t- extension compile/UI smoke tests\n872\t\n873\tNo phase is complete until its failure paths are tested.\n874\t\n875\t## Measurable Success Criteria\n876\t\n877\tThe fork should beat current Lattice on:\n878\t\n879\t- lower tool calls per coding task\n880\t- lower irrelevant context inclusion\n881\t- higher relevant file/symbol recall\n882\t- higher memory reuse precision\n883\t- lower stale memory surfacing\n884\t- better repeated-failure avoidance\n885\t- better test recommendation accuracy\n886\t- better user correction retention\n887\t- faster diagnosis from failures\n888\t- more explainable context selection\n889\t\n890\tInitial targets:\n891\t\n892\t- 30 percent fewer discovery tool calls on benchmark tasks\n893\t- 40 percent fewer irrelevant file reads\n894\t- 80 percent memory inclusion precision on curated memory benchmarks\n895\t- zero trusted display of known contradicted memory\n896\t- zero trusted display of known stale memory without stale label\n897\t- 90 percent correct relevant-test recommendation on curated tasks\n898\t\n899\t## Risks\n900\t\n901\t### Opaque Memory Mutation\n902\t\n903\tRisk: automatic consolidation corrupts durable repo knowledge.\n904\t\n905\tControl: event-backed proposals, reversible updates, provenance, review queue for high-scope changes, and replay.\n906\t\n907\t### Ranking Complexity\n908\t\n909\tRisk: ranking becomes hard to reason about.\n910\t\n911\tControl: keep ranker feature-based and inspectable before any learned policy. Expose diagnostic scores.\n912\t\n913\t### Event Log Growth\n914\t\n915\tRisk: event storage becomes too large or slow.\n916\t\n917\tControl: append compact summaries on hot paths, spill large payloads, index by task/session/workspace, and support compaction snapshots.\n918\t\n919\t### Stale Memory Leakage\n920\t\n921\tRisk: agents trust outdated memory.\n922\t\n923\tControl: freshness indexes, graph-change-triggered verification, stale labels in all memory surfaces, and tests that stale memory cannot rank as trusted.\n924\t\n925\t### Scope Leakage\n926\t\n927\tRisk: user/org/branch memories leak into wrong workspaces.\n928\t\n929\tControl: scope-aware queries, enforced filters in store APIs, and negative tests.\n930\t\n931\t### Overfitting To Current Repo\n932\t\n933\tRisk: benchmarks pass only for Lattice-like Rust/TypeScript repos.\n934\t\n935\tControl: fixture repos across languages, repo sizes, and documentation styles.\n936\t\n937\t## First Implementation Slice\n938\t\n939\tThe first real slice should be event log plus memory graph, not UI.\n940\t\n941\tImplement in this order:\n942\t\n943\t1. Event model and append-only store.\n944\t2. MCP workflow event capture.\n945\t3. Memory graph schema and migration/import from existing rows.\n946\t4. Memory access/use history.\n947\t5. Retrieval V1 with graph-aware memory ranking.\n948\t6. Compact memory inclusion reasons in existing workflow tools.\n949\t7. Session consolidation into episode memories.\n950\t8. Verification and stale surfacing tests.\n951\t\n952\tThis slice creates the substrate that all later intelligence depends on. Building UI or learned policy before this substrate would create debt instead of capability.\n953\t\n954\t## Final Target\n955\t\n956\tThe final system should make a coding agent feel like it is operating in a workspace that remembers accurately:\n957\t\n958\t- what the repo contains\n959\t- why the repo is shaped that way\n960\t- what was tried before\n961\t- what failed before\n962\t- what tests prove behavior\n963\t- what memories are trustworthy\n964\t- what memories are stale\n965\t- what context matters now\n966\t- what should be done next\n967\t\n968\tThat is the measurable leap over a code graph plus ad hoc memory store.\n969\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01LgCYkkLu5MSqvwzg2iCDvg","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"0fa88678-a6cb-4169-8c73-9d1c7278b0f0","timestamp":"2026-05-17T02:18:25.168Z","subagent_type":"Explore","task_description":"Explore docs and plans"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T03.log:2803:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:276:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RDjDdnJFRiz86tbrxskniG","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Excellent. Now let me create a comprehensive summary:\n\n## Lattice Daemon Rust Codebase Exploration Report\n\n### 1. **Crate Structure** (`daemon/crates/`)\n\n**lattice-core** (core library):\n- **graph/** — petgraph `DiGraph<GraphNode, EdgeKind>`; nodes are symbols (function/class/type), edges are `Calls|Imports|Implements|Extends|TypeRef|Contains|LinksTo|Mentions|CoChanges`\n- **parser/** — tree-sitter parsers for TypeScript, Python, Rust, Go, Java, Markdown; extracts symbols (`SymbolKind`), signatures, bodies\n- **indexer/** — incremental file-to-graph indexer; batches parse, caches in HashMap<file, ParsedFile>, rebuilds DAG when files change\n- **query/** — `QueryEngine`: intent detection, semantic + keyword search, graph traversal, ranking, budget allocation; produces `ContextCapsule` with pivot nodes and relationships\n- **storage/** — SQLite: graph persisted in `graph.db` (nodes/edges/file_index tables); file hashes cached to detect changes\n- **memory/** — SQLite-backed `MemoryStore` for observations: 80+ column schema (type, scope, verification_status, confidence, linked_symbols/files, provenance, evidence, freshness_policy, contradictions, supersedence chains)\n- **embeddings/** — ONNX embedding engine (384-dim L2-normalized vectors); loads model + tokenizer from `.lattice/models/`; mean pooling with attention mask\n- **intelligence/** — retrieval & ranking: `prepare_change`, `get_context_capsule`, `expand_context`, `plan_edit`, `trace_scenario`, `diagnose_failure`, `get_docs_capsule`, etc. (see #2)\n- **symbols/** — symbol model; stable file handles; language enum\n- **diff/** — change detection and impact analysis\n- **workspace/** — `WorkspaceManager` for multi-repo mode; detects cross-repo edges\n- **watcher/** — file system observer; ignores .git, node_modules, etc.\n- **security/** — permission filtering (likely for multi-tenant/repo access control)\n\n**lattice-daemon** (RPC server):\n- **main.rs** — entry point; loads/saves graph and memories to `.lattice/`; spawns background indexing task; initializes `QueryEngine`, `Indexer`, embedding engine, memory store; coordinates multi-repo workspace\n- **rpc/mcp.rs** — `McpHandler`: routes JSON-RPC methods to 45+ tools (see #2); manages context handles, session metrics, pruning profiles\n- **rpc/server.rs** — `StdioServer`: JSON-RPC 2.0 over stdin/stdout; async request dispatch with cancellation support; Windows binary mode\n- **rpc/protocol.rs** — JSON-RPC request/response types\n- **rpc/context_cache.rs** — in-memory cache for `ExpandContextSeed` handles to avoid re-computing context between tool calls\n- **rpc/session_metrics.rs** — per-session telemetry: tool counts, payload bytes/tokens, context reuse rates, memory writes, compact/tiny budget usage\n- **vector_sync.rs** — background task to embed file summaries into vector index\n- **watcher.rs** — file change listener; triggers incremental re-indexing\n\n---\n\n### 2. **MCP/RPC Entry Point & Tool Registration**\n\nMain entry: `StdioServer::run()` reads line-delimited JSON-RPC from stdin, delegates to `McpHandler::handle_tools_call()`.\n\n**43 registered tools** (in `mcp.rs::handle_tools_list()`):\n- **Workflow tools** (large bundles with handle-based incremental expansion):\n  - `prepare_change` — fix/add/refactor; suggests edit files, symbols, tests, risks, memory\n  - `plan_edit` — patch-oriented planning (like prepare_change, entry-point agnostic)\n  - `get_context_capsule` — unfamiliar code discovery; pivots, nearby symbols, handle\n  - `expand_context` — incremental drill-down using prior handle\n  - `get_working_set_context` — compress working set (already-open files/symbols)\n  - `trace_scenario` — execution paths, guards, side effects, failure branches\n  - `summarize_subsystem` — domain summary without full source load\n  - `impact_from_diff` — downstream impact + tests for a diff\n  - `diagnose_failure` — stack trace → suspect symbols/files/tests\n  - `record_workflow_outcome` — save successful outcomes to branch/repo memory\n\n- **Docs tools**:\n  - `get_docs_capsule` — markdown docs + linked symbols for a query\n  - `get_backlinks` — inbound references to a symbol/file/doc/section\n  - `get_outgoing_links` — outbound references from a target\n  - `find_stale_docs` — docs mentioning changed symbols/files\n  - `get_repo_playbook` — architecture, conventions, high-signal files, durable patterns\n\n- **Memory tools**:\n  - `save_observation`, `list_observations`, `search_memory`, `update_observation`, `delete_observation`, `promote_observation`, `refresh_memory`\n  - `get_session_context` — memories from current + previous sessions\n  - `list_stale_memories` — memories needing refresh\n\n- **Graph/symbol tools**:\n  - `get_skeleton` — file structure (symbols + kinds, dependent counts)\n  - `search_symbols`, `get_symbol` — by name across project\n  - `get_dependencies`, `get_dependents` — symbol's incoming/outgoing edges\n  - `get_impact_graph` — transitive dependents (N hops)\n  - `search_logic_flow` — call chains from A to B\n  - `submit_lsp_edges` — enrich graph from external LSP\n\n- **Project/workspace tools**:\n  - `get_project_rules` — project-specific conventions\n  - `workspace_setup` — language breakdown, recommended config\n  - `index_status` — indexing progress, graph stats, language breakdown\n\n- **Test tools**:\n  - `find_relevant_tests` — tests for files/symbols/diff\n\n- **Admin tools**:\n  - `get_session_metrics` — payload bytes/tokens, handle reuse, memory writes, budget mode distribution\n  - `lattice` — daemon status, graph stats, file count\n\nSession metrics expose:\n- Tool call counts per method\n- Payload bytes/tokens distribution\n- Context handle creation & reuse rates\n- Automatic memory writes & outcome patterns\n- Compact/tiny/full budget allocation counts\n- Dense wire format usage\n- Semantic fallback occurrences\n- Single-anchor task rates\n\n---\n\n### 3. **Storage Layer**\n\n**SQLite Persistence** (at `.lattice/`):\n\n1. **graph.db** — `GraphStore`\n   - Tables: `nodes` (file, name, byte_offset, kind, signature, body, line, end_line, is_exported, language, edit_count, last_modified)\n   - `edges` (from_file, from_name, from_offset, to_file, to_name, to_offset, kind)\n   - `file_index` (file, content_hash, mtime_ns, size_bytes, parser_version, schema_version, last_indexed_at)\n   - `parsed_files` (file, payload JSON)\n   - Indexes: `idx_nodes_file`, `idx_edges_from`, `idx_edges_to`\n\n2. **memories.db** — `MemoryStore`\n   - Table: `memories` (80+ columns)\n     - Core: id, session_id, content, memory_type, scope, confidence\n     - Metadata: linked_symbols, linked_files, workspace_id, branch, refresh_key, source_query\n     - Assertion shape: assertion_type (observation|decision|exploration|pattern|anti_pattern|workflow_outcome|constraint), verification_status (unverified|in_review|verified|stale|contradicted|superseded)\n     - Verification: confidence_reason, supersedes_memory_id, superseded_by_memory_id, contradicts_memory_ids, contradicted_by_memory_ids\n     - Freshness: freshness_policy (session_scoped|branch_scoped|repo_scoped), freshness_policy_detail\n     - Evidence: provenance_json, evidence_json\n     - Staleness: is_stale, stale_reason, is_invalidated\n     - Timestamps: created_at, last_accessed, access_count\n   - Full-text index: `memories_fts` (memory_id, content, linked_symbols, linked_files, unicode61 tokenizer)\n   - Indexes: by created_at, memory_type, session_id, verification_status, superseded_by\n\n3. **Vector index** — `VectorStore` (usearch-backed)\n   - File paths in `.lattice/vector_index/`\n   - Stores embeddings for symbols + file summaries (384-dim, L2-normalized)\n   - Keyed by stable file handles (hash-based identifiers)\n\n**Schema migrations**: Backward-compatible ALTER TABLE in MemoryStore::initialize() for evolving column additions.\n\n---\n\n### 4. **Memory Implementation**\n\nSQLite-backed with rich schema:\n\n**MemoryType enum**: Observation, Decision, Exploration, Pattern, AntiPattern\n\n**MemoryScope enum**: Session, Branch, Repo (durability scopes)\n\n**MemoryFreshnessPolicy enum**: SessionScoped, BranchScoped, RepoScoped (auto-staleness triggers)\n\n**MemoryVerificationStatus enum**: Unverified → InReview → Verified (terminal) OR Stale, Contradicted, Superseded\n\n**Key features**:\n- Confidence scores (0.0–1.0) with reasons\n- Contradict/supersede chains for conflict/resolution tracking\n- Provenance & evidence JSON blobs (source queries, test results, etc.)\n- Access counts for prioritization\n- FTS5 full-text search on content + symbols + files\n- Multi-session recall via query filters (session_id, scope, created_at)\n\n**Observation tools**:\n- `save_observation` (auto-generate UUID if missing)\n- `search_memory` (FTS5 + filter by scope/type/session)\n- `list_observations`, `list_stale_memories`\n- `update_observation`, `refresh_memory`, `promote_observation` (session → branch → repo)\n- `delete_observation` (soft delete via is_invalidated flag)\n\n---\n\n### 5. **Event/Observation/Telemetry Capture**\n\n**Session-level metrics** (in `session_metrics.rs`):\n- Per-tool call: payload_bytes, approx_tokens, context_handle, delivery_mode, wire_format, semantic_fallback_used, outcome_memory_reuse_count\n- Per-task: tool count, byte/token totals, delivery mode, follow-up avoidance, expand suggestions\n- Session summary: total calls, task count, byte/token aggregates, context handle reuse rate, automatic memory write count, budget distribution\n\n**Tracing**: Uses `tracing` crate; logs to stderr with EnvFilter (RUST_LOG env var); messages include:\n- Background indexing progress (file counts, graph stats)\n- Graph persistence (save/load operations)\n- Embedding engine load status\n- Cross-repo edge detection\n- Incremental indexing results (changed/removed counts)\n\n**No event pipeline or external telemetry**: metrics are in-memory per session, exposed via `get_session_metrics` RPC tool.\n\n---\n\n### 6. **Graph Implementation**\n\n**petgraph-backed**:\n- `CodeGraph` wraps `DiGraph<GraphNode, EdgeKind>` + `HashMap<SymbolId, NodeIndex>` for fast lookups\n- Nodes: `GraphNode` (id, kind, name, signature, body, file, line, end_line, is_exported, language, edit_count, last_modified)\n- Edges: 9 kinds (Calls, Imports, Implements, Extends, TypeRef, Contains, LinksTo, Mentions, CoChanges)\n\n**Parsing**:\n- tree-sitter-based per language (TypeScript, Python, Rust, Go, Java, Markdown)\n- Extracts symbol hierarchy, signatures, and edges based on language semantics\n- Caches parsed ASTs in `ParsedFile` model (file, symbols vec, symbols map)\n\n**Indexing** (`indexer.rs`):\n- `Indexer` holds `graph` + `parsed_files` HashMap\n- Incremental: parse new/changed files, replace in map, call `rebuild_graph()` once\n- `GraphBuilder` reconstructs edges from all parsed files (e.g., imports resolved by looking up imported symbol in parsed files)\n- Supports LSP edge injection via `submit_lsp_edges` to enrich graph with call hierarchy from external tools\n\n**Persistence**:\n- `GraphStore::save_graph()` serializes nodes/edges to SQLite\n- `GraphStore::load_graph()` deserializes back to `CodeGraph` on startup\n- Multi-repo mode: `WorkspaceManager` unifies graphs and detects cross-repo edges via symbol resolution\n\n---\n\n### 7. **Retrieval/Ranking Implementation**\n\nLocated in `intelligence/agent.rs` + `query/engine.rs`:\n\n**`get_context_capsule(query, mode)`**:\n1. Intent detection (keyword analysis: \"debug\"→debugging, \"docs\"→docs-first, etc.)\n2. Semantic search (embedding query, threshold ~0.65) + keyword fallback\n3. Graph traversal (1–3 hops, depending on intent)\n4. Ranking: seed hit score + semantic similarity + relationship type weight + edit recency\n5. Budget allocation: compact/full mode adjusts result counts\n6. Output: `ContextCapsule` with pivots, nearby symbols, memory highlights, suggested expand focus, handle for incremental calls\n\n**`prepare_change(query)`**:\n- `get_context_capsule` + impact graph (what breaks if changed?)\n- Recommends edit files + edit spans\n- Suggests relevant tests + risks\n- Includes linked memories (via symbol/file links)\n\n**`plan_edit(query)`**:\n- Similar to prepare_change but entry-agnostic (no prior file knowledge)\n- Bias toward likely edit files + symbols\n- Return `PlanEditBundle` with recommendations\n\n**`expand_context(handle, focus_target)`**:\n- Reuses prior context handle's index state\n- Drill down to specific file/symbol/test/memory\n- Incremental expansion (avoids recomputing everything)\n\n**`get_working_set_context(files, symbols, query)`**:\n- Compress already-open files/symbols into working set\n- Find relevant tests + nearby symbols + likely next changes\n- Budget-aware compression\n\n**`trace_scenario(scenario_description)`**:\n- \"Why does login fail after refresh?\" → execution paths from `loginUser()` → all reachable callees\n- Identify guards (if conditions), side effects, error branches\n- Return call chains + likely failure points\n\n**`summarize_subsystem(query)`**:\n- Quick overview of unfamiliar subsystem\n- Key files + symbols + tests without loading full source\n- Memory-aware (reuse prior session learnings)\n\n**`impact_from_diff(unified_diff)`**:\n- Parse diff for changed files/symbols\n- Compute transitive dependents (impact graph, N hops)\n- Suggest tests to run\n- Risk assessment (frequently edited symbols = higher risk)\n\n**`diagnose_failure(stack_trace)`**:\n- Parse trace; identify suspect symbols/files\n- Show nearby symbols + dependents (what calls the failed function?)\n- Rank by impact graph (what breaks downstream?)\n\n---\n\n### 8. **Verification Engines / Freshness Logic**\n\n**Memory verification**:\n- `MemoryVerificationStatus` enum: Unverified → InReview → Verified (or Stale/Contradicted/Superseded)\n- `MemoryFreshnessPolicy`: SessionScoped (auto-stale at session end), BranchScoped (cross-branch), RepoScoped (durable)\n- `list_stale_memories()` returns unverified or stale memories\n- `refresh_memory()` updates content + clears stale flag\n\n**Graph freshness**:\n- File index caches content_hash + mtime_ns\n- Background watcher detects file changes; incremental indexer parses + rebuidls graph once\n- Schema version + parser version tracked for detecting stale cached parses\n\n**Outcome pattern memory**:\n- `record_workflow_outcome(task, status, files, symbols, tests)` saves successful fixes/additions to branch/repo scope\n- Later workflows can reuse these patterns (e.g., \"I know how to fix login timeout because I recorded the pattern last week\")\n\n**No explicit freshness check between sessions**: relies on staleness flags + manual `promote_observation` calls to escalate session memories to branch/repo scope.\n\n---\n\n### 9. **Test Layout & Conventions**\n\n**By-module tests** (in-tree `tests.rs`):\n- `storage/tests.rs` — graph persistence, incremental indexing\n- `memory/tests.rs` — memory store CRUD, FTS5, staleness\n- `graph/tests.rs` — petgraph operations, edge kinds\n- `parser/tests.rs` — per-language parsing (Rust, TypeScript, Python, etc.)\n- `query/tests.rs` — intent detection, ranking, capsule generation\n- `indexer/tests.rs` — incremental index, graph rebuild\n- `intelligence/agent_tests.rs` — prepare_change, plan_edit, impact analysis\n- `intelligence/benchmark_tests.rs` — scaling tests (large codebases, many symbols)\n- `embeddings/tests.rs` — ONNX embedding correctness\n- `rpc/tests.rs` — JSON-RPC request/response handling\n\n**Run tests**: `cargo test --workspace` (runs all test suites in parallel)\n\n**Fixtures**: Inline test data in test functions; no external fixture files observed.\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\nLattice is a **mature, production-grade Rust codebase** with:\n- **Petgraph DAG** for inter-symbol dependencies with 9 edge types\n- **tree-sitter parsers** for 6 languages + Markdown\n- **SQLite dual-store**: graph persistence + rich memory schema (80+ columns, FTS5, contradictions, supersedence chains)\n- **ONNX embeddings**: 384-dim vectors for semantic search\n- **45 MCP tools**: workflow bundles (with handle-based incremental expansion), docs, memory, graph, tests, metrics\n- **Session telemetry**: payload tracking, budget allocation, context handle reuse, automatic memory writes\n- **Multi-repo support**: workspace manager, cross-repo edge detection\n- **Incremental indexing**: file-change detection, cached parses, graph rebuild\n\n**Recommendation**: This is **extend territory**, not fork. The foundation is complete and extensible (tools, storage schema, RPC protocol are all well-designed). Focus on integration, adding new tools, or enhancing retrieval ranking."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"39b93be1-b697-4b6a-a7fa-b2f982f99dfd","timestamp":"2026-05-17T02:20:02.891Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Explore the Lattice daemon Rust codebase at /home/pete/cadres/lattice/daemon/. Report:\n\n1. The crate structure under daemon/crates/ — list all modules and their high-level responsibilities (lattice-core and lattice-daemon).\n2. Where the main MCP/RPC server entry point lives and how MCP tools are registered.\n3. The current storage layer — what's in SQLite, how schema migrations work, file paths.\n4. The current memory implementation — schema, retrieval, observation tools.\n5. The current event/observation/telemetry capture (if any) — what's recorded, where.\n6. The current graph implementation — petgraph usage, tree-sitter parsers, indexer.\n7. The current retrieval/ranking implementation — `prepare_change`, `get_context_capsule`, etc.\n8. Verification engines or freshness logic that exist today.\n9. Test layout and conventions (cargo test --workspace, fixtures).\n\nCite file paths and key types. Be precise about what exists vs what doesn't. Report in under 800 words; this is groundwork for a build plan that decides whether to fork or extend.","agentId":"a99faae06255f410a","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Excellent. Now let me create a comprehensive summary:\n\n## Lattice Daemon Rust Codebase Exploration Report\n\n### 1. **Crate Structure** (`daemon/crates/`)\n\n**lattice-core** (core library):\n- **graph/** — petgraph `DiGraph<GraphNode, EdgeKind>`; nodes are symbols (function/class/type), edges are `Calls|Imports|Implements|Extends|TypeRef|Contains|LinksTo|Mentions|CoChanges`\n- **parser/** — tree-sitter parsers for TypeScript, Python, Rust, Go, Java, Markdown; extracts symbols (`SymbolKind`), signatures, bodies\n- **indexer/** — incremental file-to-graph indexer; batches parse, caches in HashMap<file, ParsedFile>, rebuilds DAG when files change\n- **query/** — `QueryEngine`: intent detection, semantic + keyword search, graph traversal, ranking, budget allocation; produces `ContextCapsule` with pivot nodes and relationships\n- **storage/** — SQLite: graph persisted in `graph.db` (nodes/edges/file_index tables); file hashes cached to detect changes\n- **memory/** — SQLite-backed `MemoryStore` for observations: 80+ column schema (type, scope, verification_status, confidence, linked_symbols/files, provenance, evidence, freshness_policy, contradictions, supersedence chains)\n- **embeddings/** — ONNX embedding engine (384-dim L2-normalized vectors); loads model + tokenizer from `.lattice/models/`; mean pooling with attention mask\n- **intelligence/** — retrieval & ranking: `prepare_change`, `get_context_capsule`, `expand_context`, `plan_edit`, `trace_scenario`, `diagnose_failure`, `get_docs_capsule`, etc. (see #2)\n- **symbols/** — symbol model; stable file handles; language enum\n- **diff/** — change detection and 