ct Properties\n102:## System Architecture\n104:### 1. Ingestion Layer\n131:### 2. Workspace Graph\n172:### 3. Event Log\n214:### 4. Memory Graph\n285:### 5. Working Memory\n316:### 6. Consolidation Engine\n354:### 7. Retrieval Engine\n401:### 8. Verification Engine\n430:### 9. MCP Surface\n476:### 10. Human Review Surface\n496:## Storage Design\n538:## Fork Strategy\n564:## Implementation Plan\n566:### Phase 0: Fork Foundation\n593:### Phase 1: Unified Identity Model\n611:### Phase 2: Event Log Substrate\n631:### Phase 3: Memory Graph Storage\n649:### Phase 4: Retrieval V1\n669:### Phase 5: Working Memory\n684:### Phase 6: Consolidation Engine\n705:### Phase 7: Verification And Freshness\n722:### Phase 8: Workflow Engine V2\n736:### Phase 9: Metrics And Evaluation\n765:### Phase 10: Human Review And Extension UX\n784:### Phase 11: Hardening\n802:## MCP Tool Contract Principles\n836:## Documentation Requirements\n854:## Testing Requirements\n875:## Measurable Success Criteria\n899:## Risks\n901:### Opaque Memory Mutation\n907:### Ranking Complexity\n913:### Event Log Growth\n919:### Stale Memory Leakage\n925:### Scope Leakage\n931:### Overfitting To Current Repo\n937:## First Implementation Slice\n954:## Final Target","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/R33.log:253:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BtKKhgbn97Dp8AqdY3FpWb","type":"tool_result","content":"354\t### 7. Retrieval Engine\n355\t\n356\tRetrieval must be task-aware and explainable.\n357\t\n358\tPipeline:\n359\t\n360\t1. Parse user task and classify intent.\n361\t2. Extract literal anchors: paths, symbols, errors, commands, APIs, config keys.\n362\t3. Resolve anchors into graph identities.\n363\t4. Retrieve graph candidates.\n364\t5. Retrieve memory candidates from typed streams.\n365\t6. Retrieve relevant event episodes.\n366\t7. Expand through bounded graph and memory neighborhoods.\n367\t8. Score candidates.\n368\t9. Deduplicate and compress.\n369\t10. Return a compact bundle with inclusion reasons and expansion handles.\n370\t\n371\tCandidate sources:\n372\t\n373\t- exact path/symbol lookup\n374\t- code graph traversal\n375\t- doc backlinks/outgoing links\n376\t- FTS\n377\t- embeddings\n378\t- event similarity\n379\t- memory links\n380\t- workflow similarity\n381\t- recent active working memory\n382\t\n383\tRanking signals:\n384\t\n385\t- task-type compatibility\n386\t- graph proximity to anchors\n387\t- exact identifier match\n388\t- semantic similarity\n389\t- verification status\n390\t- freshness\n391\t- scope\n392\t- evidence strength\n393\t- contradiction/supersession state\n394\t- past usefulness\n395\t- recent successful reuse\n396\t- user preference compatibility\n397\t- token cost\n398\t\n399\tThe ranker should expose scores and reasons in diagnostic mode. Compact mode should include only enough explanation for an assistant to trust the result.\n400\t\n401\t### 8. Verification Engine\n402\t\n403\tThe verification engine keeps memory honest."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6bc825b2-855a-40f4-bda9-b6466b178977","uuid":"49d5d6b6-2c52-419c-8828-b3a8cdcb6693","timestamp":"2026-05-17T09:20:59.363Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md","content":"### 7. Retrieval Engine\n\nRetrieval must be task-aware and explainable.\n\nPipeline:\n\n1. Parse user task and classify intent.\n2. Extract literal anchors: paths, symbols, errors, commands, APIs, config keys.\n3. Resolve anchors into graph identities.\n4. Retrieve graph candidates.\n5. Retrieve memory candidates from typed streams.\n6. Retrieve relevant event episodes.\n7. Expand through bounded graph and memory neighborhoods.\n8. Score candidates.\n9. Deduplicate and compress.\n10. Return a compact bundle with inclusion reasons and expansion handles.\n\nCandidate sources:\n\n- exact path/symbol lookup\n- code graph traversal\n- doc backlinks/outgoing links\n- FTS\n- embeddings\n- event similarity\n- memory links\n- workflow similarity\n- recent active working memory\n\nRanking signals:\n\n- task-type compatibility\n- graph proximity to anchors\n- exact identifier match\n- semantic similarity\n- verification status\n- freshness\n- scope\n- evidence strength\n- contradiction/supersession state\n- past usefulness\n- recent successful reuse\n- user preference compatibility\n- token cost\n\nThe ranker should expose scores and reasons in diagnostic mode. Compact mode should include only enough explanation for an assistant to trust the result.\n\n### 8. Verification Engine\n\nThe verification engine keeps memory honest.","numLines":50,"startLine":354,"totalLines":969}}}
