odels/`; vector store backed by USearch at `.lattice/vector_index/`.\n42\t- **Intelligence:** workflow tools `prepare_change`, `plan_edit`, `get_context_capsule`, `expand_context`, `get_working_set_context`, `trace_scenario`, `summarize_subsystem`, `impact_from_diff`, `diagnose_failure`, `record_workflow_outcome`.\n43\t- **Structured memory:** verification status (`unverified` → `in_review` → `verified` / `stale` / `contradicted` / `superseded`), freshness policy (`session_scoped` | `branch_scoped` | `repo_scoped`), assertion types, provenance/evidence JSON blobs — documented in `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-structured-memory.md`.\n44\t- **Stable handles:** `SymbolId { file, name, byte_offset }` already used by `expand_context` — documented in `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-stable-follow-up-handles.md`.\n45\t\n46\t### MCP / RPC\n47\t\n48\t- JSON-RPC 2.0 over stdio (`crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/server.rs`).\n49\t- 43 registered tools dispatched through `McpHandler::handle_tools_call()` (`rpc/mcp.rs`).\n50\t- Context-handle cache (`rpc/context_cache.rs`) and per-session telemetry (`rpc/session_metrics.rs`).\n51\t\n52\t### Extension (TypeScript)\n53\t\n54\t- `extension/src/`: `extension.ts` (activation + 14 commands), `daemon.ts` (RPC client), `sidebar.ts` (webview with delivery-mix + handle-reuse meters), `docsWorkbench.ts` (docs graph), `codelens.ts`, `hover.ts`, `statusbar.ts`.\n55\t- Compile via `cd extension && npm run compile`; lint via `npm run lint`; tests via `npm test` (`node ./out/test/runTest.js`).\n56\t- Activity bar contribution (`viewsContainers.activitybar.lattice`) is the slot the new review UI will extend.\n57\t\n58\t### Tests\n59\t\n60\t- `cd daemon && cargo test --workspace` runs all crates.\n61\t- In-tree `tests.rs` next to each module is the convention (e.g. `storage/tests.rs`, `memory/tests.rs`, `intelligence/agent_tests.rs`).\n62\t- Benchmark fixtures live under `crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/benchmark_tests.rs` and `crates/lattice-core/src/query/benchmark_tests.rs`.\n63\t\n64\t### Architecture docs already present\n65\t\n66\t- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-structured-memory.md`\n67\t- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-stable-follow-up-handles.md`\n68\t- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-patch-oriented-planning.md`\n69\t- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-scenario-tracing.md`\n70\t- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-storage-and-search-backends.md`\n71\t\n72\tReference these from new design notes; do not duplicate their content.\n73\t\n74\t---\n75\t\n76\t## 3. Gap inventory (what the spec needs that does not yet exist)\n77\t\n78\t| Spec section | Status in current codebase | Build action |\n79\t|---|---|---|\n80\t| **Phase 0** — fork-or-extend decision + baseline benchmarks | Not done | Decision doc, baseline benchmark capture |\n81\t| **Phase 1** — unified identity for files, docs, sections, events, memories, handles | Partial: only `SymbolId` is stable today | Generalize identity, add resolver, ambiguity diagnostics |\n82\t| **Phase 2** — append-only event log + 20 event kinds + compaction | Absent | New `events` + `event_payloads` tables, writer/reader API, MCP capture, compaction snapshots |\n83\t| **Phase 3** — memory graph with first-class links, evidence, accesses, scores; `CounterMemory` class | Memory is single-table today | New `memory_links`, `memory_evidence`, `memory_accesses`, `memory_scores`; class taxonomy expansion; migration from existing rows |\n84\t| **Phase 4** — Retrieval V1 with intent classifier, anchor resolver, hybrid candidates, explainable scoring | Heuristic intent + keyword/semantic in `query/engine.rs`; no diagnostic reasons | New retrieval pipeline returning inclusion reasons + expansion handles |\n85\t| **Phase 5** — explicit working memory + checkpoints | Implicit prompt assembly today | New `working_memory_checkpoints` table + operations + MCP surface |\n86\t| **Phase 6** — consolidation engine (deterministic + LLM-driven proposals, manual queue, replay-safe) | Absent | New `consolidation_jobs` table, proposal-only writes, LLM provenance |\n87\t| **Phase 7** — verification engine (existence, span, scope, expiry, incremental) | `is_stale` flag exists; no incremental verifier | New verifier + `verification_jobs`; scope-leak prevention; stale-label discipline |\n88\t| **Phase 8** — Workflow Engine V2 + 10 new memory tools (audited for surface size) | Old tools exist; new memory tools absent | Redesign existing tools + add audited memory toolset |\n89\t| **Phase 9** — metrics + golden benchmark suite | Session metrics exist; no golden tasks | New benchmark fixture repo + CLI report |\n90\t| **Phase 10** — Human Review UI (memory inbox, queues, event/retrieval views) | Absent (sidebar shows stats only) | New webviews + JSON-RPC plumbing |\n91\t| **Phase 11** — hardening + full documentation suite | Partial | Large-repo perf, concurrency, recovery, corruption, migration tests; complete doc suite |\n92\t\n93\t---\n94\t\n95\t## 4. Fork-or-extend decision (spec §Phase 0 gate)\n96\t\n97\tThe spec's gate conditions for forking are: breaking `memories` table changes incompatible with migration, identity primary-key changes invalidating indexes, event log requiring incompatible SQLite/WAL changes, or two-or-more of the above.\n98\t\n99\tCurrent evidence suggests **extend on a long-lived branch** is feasible:\n100\t\n101\t- The existing `memories` schema already accommodates most of the new fields; new tables (`memory_links`, `memory_evidence`, `memory_accesses`, `memory_scores`) can be added alongside.\n102\t- `SymbolId` already gives stable identity; generalizing to other node families is additive.\n103\t- The event log can be a new SQLite file (`.lattice/events.db`) without disturbing `graph.db` or `memories.db`.\n104\t\n105\t**The fork-or-extend decision is itself the first task (T01).** It is binding for every subsequent task: `depends_on` chains assume the decision documents which repo layout, crate boundaries, and migration policy all subsequent work targets. The harness does not assume the answer.\n106\t\n107\t---\n108\t\n109\t## 5. Shared standards every task must satisfy\n110\t\n111\t| Standard | Location | Where it applies |\n112\t|---|---|---|\n113\t| Cadres coding standard | `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md` | All code tasks: hard limits (file ≤800 lines, function ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10), single source of truth, error handling, naming, no broken windows, schema parity, tests as documentation, observability. |\n114\t| UI specification | `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/ui-specification.md` | All frontend tasks (Phase 10 review UI): every interactive surface follows the spec; no `window.confirm/prompt/alert`, modals via `<dialog>`, i18n via `i18n.t(...)`. |\n115\t| Definition of done | `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/definition-of-done-checklist.md` | The final readiness review task asserts every applicable checklist item is satisfied or explicitly waived. |\n116\t| Lattice project rules | `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/AGENTS.md` | Build/deploy procedure (`pkill -f lattice && cp daemon/target/release/lattice extension/bin/ && cp daemon/target/release/lattice ~/.vscode/extensions/lattice.lattice-0.1.0/bin/`), Markdown heading references for doc-anchored behavior, backward-compatibility discipline. |\n117\t| Execution philosophy | `/home/pete/.claude/CLAUDE.md` \"Execution Philosophy\" | No deferrals, no workarounds, no half-finished work, enterprise-grade on day one. The fork is implemented end-to-end across all eleven phases. |\n118\t\n119\t---\n120\t\n121\t## 6. Verification discipline\n122\t\n123\tEvery task in `tasks.json` carries `verification_commands` that are real shell commands run from the repo root. Conventions:\n124\t\n125\t- **Doc tasks** verify file existence and presence of required headings via `test -f` + `grep -q \"## Heading\"` chains.\n126\t- **Backend tasks** verify with `cd daemon && cargo build --release` and `cd daemon && cargo test --workspace -- <scope>`. New crate or module tests are pinned to the precise test path (`cargo test -p lattice-core --lib events::tests`) so failures map to a single task.\n127\t- **Schema/migration tasks** include a `cargo test` invocation that exercises the migration on a temp DB, plus a `sqlite3` schema-dump diff against a checked-in golden schema where the spec demands it.\n128\t- **Frontend tasks** verify with `cd extension && npm run compile` and `npm run lint`. Smoke tasks add `npm test`.\n129\t- **Cross-layer contract gates** include MCP schema tests (`cargo test --workspace -- mcp_schema`) and extension/daemon round-trip tests that exercise the new tool surface end-to-end.\n130\t- **Benchmark tasks** include `cargo bench` or a CLI invocation that emits a metrics report file the verifier can `test -f`.\n131\t- **Review tasks** verify by file existence of a structured findings report under `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/RNN.md` with the required sections.\n132\t\n133\tIf a task's verification cannot be expressed as a real command, the task is split or rewritten until it can.\n134\t\n135\t---\n136\t\n137\t## 7. Sequencing rationale\n138\t\n139\tThe eleven spec phases collapse into the following review groupings:\n140\t\n141\t1. **Foundation review** after Phase 0 — fork/extend decision, baseline benchmarks, crate boundary plan, architecture overview.\n142\t2. **Backend reviews** after Phases 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8 — one per substrate layer.\n143\t3. **Contract gates** at three boundaries: (a) Identity + Event Log + Memory cross-layer, (b) Memory + Verification + Retrieval cross-layer, (c) MCP tool surface schema regression, (d) Extension ↔ Daemon review-UI plumbing.\n144\t4. **Metrics review** after Phase 9.\n145\t5. **Frontend review** after Phase 10.\n146\t6. **Tests + Docs review** after Phase 11.\n147\t7. **Definition-of-Done gate** asserting every shared standard is satisfied.\n148\t8. **Final readiness review** asserting the system is production-grade for repeated agent use.\n149\t\n150\tNo phase is allowed to defer scope from a later phase. No phase ships a workaround when the real implementation is reachable in the same build.\n151\t\n152\t---\n153\t\n154\t## 8. Out of scope (explicitly named so it is not silently dropped)\n155\t\n156\tThe spec lists no items as out of scope. Therefore every section under spec §Implementation Plan, §Storage Design, §MCP Tool Contract Principles, §Documentation Requirements, §Testing Requirements, §Measurable Success Criteria, and §Risks is in scope for this build. The first-implementation-slice ordering is a *priority hint*, not a scope reduction.\n157\t\n158\tThe plan honors that: phases 0 through 11 are each materialized as tasks. The \"First Implementation Slice\" steps map to the earliest tasks in their respective phases — they are sequenced first inside their phase, but every phase ships in full before the build completes.\n159\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XjrTa6YAS1dmMW989ZPauZ","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"2286115f-f5be-472c-a8a6-d87768c1eefa","timestamp":"2026-05-17T02:33:24.491Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 10+11+Final task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T41.log:1959:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2726:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:711:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_016MBNff4Vmm8f1myrXiWNYC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WwRjPQsgfzTC4J4X1QNmPX","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T59.md","content":"# T59 — Phase 8 — New memory tools: verify_memory + explain_memory (unified) + list_memory_conflicts\n\n**Phase:** 8\n**Type:** backend\n**Model class:** balanced\n**Depends on:** R55\n**Spec anchor:** [§MCP Surface](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#9-mcp-surface)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard; Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task introduces the unified `verify_explain_memory` MCP tool (collapsing the spec's `verify_memory` + `explain_memory` per §MCP Surface \"Tool surface discipline\") and the new `list_memory_conflicts` tool. The unified tool runs verification checks against a memory and returns both the resulting status (`verified` | `unverified` | `in_review` | `stale` | `contradicted` | `superseded` | `expired` | `invalidated`) and a structured explanation of why each linked file/symbol/doc/test contributed to that status. `list_memory_conflicts` surfaces all contradiction and supersession edges anchored on a target memory or graph node so an operator can review them. Both tools are required by spec §MCP Surface and are the trust surface for the verification engine — without them, the Phase 7 verifier produces statuses that no assistant or operator can interrogate.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"New or redesigned memory tools: …`verify_memory`, `explain_memory`, `list_memory_conflicts`…\" (spec §MCP Surface)\n\n> \"Tool surface discipline: …audit whether `verify_memory` + `explain_memory` can be unified. The goal is the smallest surface that covers all assistant workflows. Consolidate before stabilizing the MCP contract.\" (spec §MCP Surface)\n\n> \"Verification outputs: `verified`, `unverified`, `in_review`, `stale`, `contradicted`, `superseded`, `expired`, `invalidated`.\" (spec §Verification Engine)\n\n> \"Every memory-bearing response should identify: why the memory was included; evidence strength; verification status; freshness status; contradiction/supersession state; scope; expansion target.\" (spec §MCP Tool Contract Principles)\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs: R55 (Phase 7 verification engine review) — `verify_explain_memory` is the MCP surface for the Phase 7 verifier; its outputs must match the Phase 7 status taxonomy exactly.\n- Existing repo files this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/verification/` — Phase 7 verifier (existence, span, scope, expiry checks; incremental verification jobs).\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory/` — Phase 3 memory store + links + evidence + accesses + scores.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/` — Phase 2 event writer (emits `MemoryRetrieved`, `MemoryInvalidated`).\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs` — MCP tool registration.\n  - Phase 1 identity types.\n  - T58 shared `MemoryRecord` type.\n- Standards: Cadres coding standard §Hard limits, §Single source of truth, §No broken windows, §Schema parity, §Observability; Lattice rules on Markdown heading references; `AGENTS.md` backward-compatibility discipline.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read spec §MCP Surface and §Verification Engine in full. Quote the \"Tool surface discipline\" paragraph verbatim into a doc comment on `memory_v2/verify_explain_memory.rs` so the rationale for unifying `verify_memory` + `explain_memory` is preserved at the call site.\n2. Implement `verify_explain_memory` in `memory_v2/verify_explain_memory.rs` as the unified tool. Accept `memory_id: MemoryId` plus optional `mode: \"verify\" | \"explain\" | \"verify_and_explain\"` (default `verify_and_explain`). Internally run the Phase 7 verifier on the memory, then construct an `ExplainReport` that itemizes each verification check (linked file exists, linked symbol exists, cited doc exists, linked test exists, evidence span still matches, contradiction state coherent, scope leak absent, time-bound expiry honored) and the outcome per check. Return a `VerifyExplainResponse` with `status: VerificationStatus`, `checks: Vec<CheckResult>` (each carrying `kind`, `target`, `outcome`, `evidence_ref`), `confidence_delta`, and an `expansion_handle` so the caller can pull richer context.\n3. Mode semantics: `verify` returns only the status + a minimal summary; `explain` skips re-running checks and returns the last persisted `ExplainReport`; `verify_and_explain` runs verification and returns the freshly-derived `ExplainReport`. Default is `verify_and_explain` because spec §Verification Engine and §MCP Tool Contract Principles together require fresh, explainable status on every memory surface.\n4. Implement `list_memory_conflicts` in `memory_v2/list_memory_conflicts.rs`. Accept `anchor: MemoryId | FileId | SymbolId | DocSectionId`, traverse `contradicts` and `supersedes` link types from Phase 3 memory_links, and return a paginated list of `ConflictRecord`s each carrying `source`, `target`, `link_type` (`contradicts` | `supersedes`), `link_strength`, `created_by`, `created_at`, `link_verification_status`, and `reason`. Honor scope filters per spec §Risks \"Scope Leakage.\"\n5. Both tools must emit appropriate Phase 2 events: `verify_explain_memory` emits `MemoryRetrieved` plus `MemoryInvalidated` when status transitions away from `verified`; `list_memory_conflicts` emits `MemoryRetrieved` for each surfaced conflict.\n6. Both tools must support compact + full + diagnostic render modes per spec §MCP Tool Contract Principles. Compact mode for `verify_explain_memory` returns status + one-line per failed check; diagnostic mode returns the full check trace.\n7. Register the two tools in `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs`. Per the T02 compatibility policy, the spec's nominal `verify_memory` and `explain_memory` names continue to respond as deprecation shims that forward to `verify_explain_memory` with the appropriate `mode`. The shims emit a deprecation warning in the response.\n8. In `memory_v2/verify_explain_tests.rs`, write tests that: (a) round-trip each tool's request/response through serde; (b) assert `verify_explain_memory` returns the exact Phase 7 status taxonomy and never invents a new status string; (c) assert every check result carries an evidence reference; (d) assert a memory whose linked symbol was deleted transitions to `stale` with a `linked_symbol_missing` check failure; (e) assert `list_memory_conflicts` returns both `contradicts` and `supersedes` edges with correct directionality; (f) assert scope-leak negative tests; (g) assert the `verify_memory` and `explain_memory` deprecation shims continue to respond and forward correctly.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/memory_v2/verify_explain_memory.rs` — unified verify+explain tool with `mode` parameter, full check trace, and expansion handle, per spec §MCP Surface tool-surface discipline.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/memory_v2/list_memory_conflicts.rs` — `list_memory_conflicts` traversing contradiction + supersession links with scope-aware pagination.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/memory_v2/verify_explain_tests.rs` — unit + scope-leak negative tests covering schema, status taxonomy parity with Phase 7, evidence-reference completeness, conflict directionality, and shim forwarding.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/memory_v2/verify_explain_memory.rs` — confirms the unified verify+explain file exists at the harness path.\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/memory_v2/list_memory_conflicts.rs` — confirms the `list_memory_conflicts` file exists at the harness path.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-daemon --lib rpc::memory_v2::verify_explain_tests` — every verify/explain/conflicts test passes (schema, status parity, evidence completeness, conflict directionality, scope-leak negatives, shim forwarding).\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file.\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10.\n- Schema parity: `VerifyExplainResponse`, `CheckResult`, `ConflictRecord` derive `Serialize`, `Deserialize`, `Debug`, `Clone`; every public field has a doc comment; the `mode` enum is serde-tagged with snake_case wire names; the `VerificationStatus` enum reuses the Phase 7 type rather than declaring a parallel one.\n- No silent error handling — every verifier failure returns a typed error; scope-leak rejections are explicit errors, not empty results.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code; no unjustified `#[allow(...)]`.\n- Public API names follow intent-over-mechanism naming (`CheckResult::evidence_ref`, not `CheckResult::ref_str`).\n- For SQLite: all queries go through Phase 3 store and Phase 7 verifier APIs; no raw SQL in the RPC layer.\n- Observability: each tool emits `tracing::info!` spans with `tool`, `memory_id`, `scope`, `status`, `check_count` fields.\n- Doc citation: cite spec headings `## MCP Surface`, `## Verification Engine`, and `## MCP Tool Contract Principles` in module doc comments.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: the unified `verify_explain_memory` ships in this task; the spec's two-tool API is collapsed before MCP contract stabilization per §MCP Surface.\n- [ ] If a backward-incompatible change was needed, the AGENTS.md \"Backward-compatibility and contract discipline\" section was followed; the `verify_memory` + `explain_memory` deprecation shims continue to respond per the T02 compatibility policy.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4764,"cache_read_input_tokens":68448,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4764,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":59,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":{"cache_miss_reason":{"type":"unavailable"}},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012n7S8LWZzY1cvitTHPzudV","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"6925f9b8-658b-4628-9f96-5512a5babaa4","request_id":"req_011Cb7MzXVz1xvApfw62RZzy","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 8+9+Gate task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T41.log:1974:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2741:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:806:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_015fKcvZfgcEs97BQgrrZ5xL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Y7QFPHstAz9FnuCrEEM8Ld","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T65.md","content":"# T65 — Phase 9 — Metric collection module (all required signals from spec)\n\n**Phase:** 9\n**Type:** backend\n**Model class:** balanced\n**Depends on:** R64\n**Spec anchor:** [§Phase 9: Metrics And Evaluation](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-9-metrics-and-evaluation)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard; Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task creates the canonical metrics module that collects every assistant-usefulness signal required by spec §Phase 9. Without a single module owning these signals, downstream tasks (T66 benchmark fixtures, T67 MCP surface, T68 CLI report, T69 regression tests) would each invent their own definitions and diverge. The module turns the event log + memory access history + workflow outcomes into the nine required signals named in spec §Phase 9: tool calls per successful task, irrelevant files opened per task, relevant anchor recall, memory inclusion precision, memory later-used rate, stale memory surfaced rate, contradiction missed rate, tests recommended versus tests needed, and workflow success after first plan.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Required metrics: tool calls per successful task; irrelevant files opened per task; relevant anchor recall; memory inclusion precision; memory later-used rate; stale memory surfaced rate; contradiction missed rate; tests recommended versus tests needed; workflow success after first plan.\" (spec §Phase 9)\n\n> \"Phase 9 Deliverables: retrieval relevance metrics; memory usefulness metrics; context token efficiency metrics; stale/contradicted memory surfacing metrics; test-pass-after-plan metrics; repeated-failure reduction metrics; benchmark tasks with golden expected anchors; regression dashboard or CLI report.\"\n\n> \"Definition of done: changes to retrieval, memory, and workflow behavior can be evaluated without relying on anecdote.\" (spec §Phase 9)\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs: R64 (MCP contract gate) — metric collection depends on a stable tool surface so signal definitions reference canonical tool names from T61.\n- Existing repo files this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/` — Phase 2 event reader; all metrics derive from event aggregation.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory/` — Phase 3 memory accesses, scores, evidence; used for memory-related signals.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/verification/` — Phase 7 verification statuses; used for stale-surfaced and contradiction-missed signals.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/session_metrics.rs` — current per-session telemetry; T65 supersedes its partial coverage with a complete spec-aligned set.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/record_workflow_outcome.rs` and the T62 outcome recorder — source for workflow success signals.\n  - Phase 1 identity types (every signal references stable IDs, never names).\n- Standards: Cadres coding standard `## Hard limits`, `## Single source of truth`, `## No broken windows`, `## Schema / model parity`, `## Observability`, `## Tests as documentation`.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read spec §Phase 9 in full. Quote the \"Required metrics\" paragraph verbatim into a doc comment on `metrics/signals.rs` so the canonical list is preserved at the call site.\n2. Create `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/metrics/mod.rs` exporting `signals`, the `MetricSignal` enum, the `MetricValue` struct, and the `MetricsCollector` aggregator. Doc-comment the module with a reference to spec §Phase 9.\n3. In `metrics/signals.rs`, define `pub enum MetricSignal` with one variant per spec-required signal: `ToolCallsPerSuccessfulTask`, `IrrelevantFilesOpenedPerTask`, `RelevantAnchorRecall`, `MemoryInclusionPrecision`, `MemoryLaterUsedRate`, `StaleMemorySurfacedRate`, `ContradictionMissedRate`, `TestsRecommendedVsNeeded`, `WorkflowSuccessAfterFirstPlan`. Derive `Serialize`, `Deserialize`, `Clone`, `Copy`, `Debug`, `PartialEq`, `Eq`, `Hash`. Implement `MetricSignal::as_str()` returning the canonical snake_case wire name used by the T67 MCP surface and the T68 CLI report.\n4. Define `pub struct MetricValue { signal: MetricSignal, value: Option<f64>, denominator: Option<u64>, sample_count: u64, source: MetricSource, computed_at: DateTime<Utc>, reason_if_null: Option<String> }`. The `Option<f64>` value supports honest reporting when no data is available — never fabricate zeros. The `source` field discriminates `EventLog`, `MemoryStore`, `WorkflowOutcome`, `Verifier`, `SessionMetrics`.\n5. In `metrics/signals.rs`, implement a deterministic collector function per signal that aggregates from the appropriate source(s):\n   - `ToolCallsPerSuccessfulTask` — count `ToolCalled` events per `task_id` whose terminal event is `WorkflowSucceeded`; mean across tasks in scope.\n   - `IrrelevantFilesOpenedPerTask` — count `FileRead` events per task where the file was not part of the eventual `PatchApplied` or `WorkflowSucceeded` artifact set.\n   - `RelevantAnchorRecall` — fraction of golden anchors (from T66 fixtures) that appear in the workflow's `ContextBundleReturned` anchor list.\n   - `MemoryInclusionPrecision` — fraction of `MemoryRetrieved` entries that were subsequently referenced (`MemoryExpanded`, cited by `PatchApplied`, or referenced by a later `WorkflowSucceeded`).\n   - `MemoryLaterUsedRate` — fraction of retrieved memories that the consumer used downstream within the same session or a subsequent reuse window.\n   - `StaleMemorySurfacedRate` — fraction of `MemoryRetrieved` entries whose verification status was `stale` and that lacked a stale label in the response payload.\n   - `ContradictionMissedRate` — fraction of memory inclusions where a contradiction link existed but was not surfaced.\n   - `TestsRecommendedVsNeeded` — comparison between `find_relevant_tests` output and the test set actually required to detect the patch (golden answer from T66).\n   - `WorkflowSuccessAfterFirstPlan` — fraction of tasks where the first `PlanCreated` event led to `WorkflowSucceeded` without a `WorkflowFailed` in between.\n6. Implement `pub struct MetricsCollector { … }` exposing `collect(scope: MetricScope, signals: &[MetricSignal]) -> Vec<MetricValue>`. `MetricScope` mirrors the §Memory Graph scope taxonomy (`session | branch | repo | user | organization`) plus an optional time range.\n7. Honor the spec §Non-Negotiable Product Properties bounds: no unbounded event-log scan on hot paths. Every collector applies a scope filter and a configurable maximum sample size, with explicit `incomplete: true` on the `MetricValue` when truncated.\n8. In `metrics/signals_tests.rs`, write tests that: (a) every `MetricSignal` variant has a deterministic collector; (b) collectors return `None` with a reason when no data is available rather than fabricating zero; (c) each collector returns the spec-defined value on a hand-crafted event fixture; (d) scope filters prevent cross-scope leakage; (e) sample-size bounds are honored.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/metrics/mod.rs` — module root exporting `signals`, `MetricSignal`, `MetricValue`, `MetricSource`, `MetricsCollector`, `MetricScope`; doc-comment cites spec §Phase 9.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/metrics/signals.rs` — `MetricSignal` enum with all nine spec-required variants, `MetricValue` struct, deterministic collector implementations, scope and sample-size bounds.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/metrics/signals_tests.rs` — unit tests covering deterministic collectors, honest null reporting, fixture-derived correct values, scope-filter enforcement, sample-size bounds.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/metrics/mod.rs` — confirms the module root file exists at the harness path.\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/metrics/signals.rs` — confirms the signals file exists at the harness path.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-core --lib metrics::signals_tests` — every metric-signal test passes (deterministic collectors, honest null, fixture correctness, scope filters, sample-size bounds).\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file (split `signals.rs` per signal family if it grows beyond, e.g. `signals/tool_usage.rs`, `signals/memory.rs`).\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10.\n- Schema parity: `MetricSignal`, `MetricValue`, `MetricSource`, `MetricScope` derive `Serialize`, `Deserialize`, `Debug`, `Clone`; every public field has a doc comment; wire names match the strings used by T67/T68.\n- No silent error handling — missing data produces `None` with a `reason_if_null` string, never a fabricated zero.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code; no unjustified `#[allow(...)]`.\n- Public API names follow intent-over-mechanism naming (`MetricSignal::RelevantAnchorRecall`, not `MetricSignal::AnchorRec`).\n- For SQLite: queries go through Phase 2/3/7 APIs; no raw SQL in the metrics module.\n- Observability: collectors emit `tracing::debug!` spans with `signal`, `scope`, `sample_count`, `incomplete` fields.\n- Doc citation: cite spec headings `## Phase 9: Metrics And Evaluation`, `## Event Log`, `## Memory Graph`, `## Verification Engine` in module doc comments.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: every one of the nine spec §Phase 9 \"Required metrics\" signals is collected by this task — no signal is punted to T67 or T68.\n- [ ] If a backward-incompatible change was needed, the AGENTS.md \"Backward-compatibility and contract discipline\" section was followed; the existing `session_metrics` module retains its current API as a deprecation shim until T67 wires the canonical surface through MCP.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3516,"cache_read_input_tokens":92137,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3516,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":51,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012n7S8LWZzY1cvitTHPzudV","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"3cf115cb-be58-4e98-bfce-c64ff2fe93eb","request_id":"req_011Cb7NM9YiaUaTnV73t6JP7","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 8+9+Gate task briefs"}
